module ntasm_macro_expand;

import ntasm_parser;
import ntasm_ast;
import constant_eval;

// Bounded hygienic expansion of NTASM v0 macro declarations.
//
// spec/ntasm/ntasm.md 13: a macro receives and returns typed AST nodes, never a
// string to re-inject in the parser; hygiene applies a fresh deterministic
// mark to every introduced symbol; the capture of a caller symbol is refused
// by default; the six limits of 12 are the same as for comptime; a macro that
// exceeds fuel, memory, nodes or depth fails without a usable partial AST;
// the expansion order is lexical and stable.
//
// The hygiene marks reproduce compiler/c/ntasm/frontend/frontend.c exactly:
//   let local        __ntm$<ordinal>$e<serial>$<name>     frontend.c:2384
//   declaration      __ntd$<ordinal>$e<serial>$<name>     frontend.c:4818
//   emitted list item
//                    __ntd$<o>$e<s>$r<repeat>$t<item>$<n> frontend.c:4797
//   fn parameter     __ntp$<ordinal>$e<serial>$p<i>$<n>  frontend.c:4938
// <ordinal> is the one based declaration order of the macro in the module and
// <serial> is incremented once per expansion, before any mark is built.

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ limits
// The frozen maxima of frontend.h:55-62. A comptime block may lower any of
// them but never raise one.
fn max_fuel()->u64 { return 1048576; }
fn max_call_depth()->u64 { return 256; }
fn max_arena()->u64 { return 16777216; }
fn max_ast_nodes()->u64 { return 1048576; }
fn max_expansion_depth()->u64 { return 128; }
fn max_constant_bytes()->u64 { return 16777216; }
// The eight declared bounds, and what each one is now. Before this module
// read node words and spellings directly, FIVE of the SEVEN it had then were
// shadowed: nothing reached them, because the host buffer table filled first
// and the expander published nothing at all when it did. That shadow is
// gone, so the answer is no longer "the bound exists" -- it is per bound, and
// a bound that nothing can reach says so HERE rather than letting a caller
// read it as a guarantee. All eight are ENFORCED in code. What differs is
// whether a body can get there, and that is measured, not asserted.
//
// REACHABLE. The refusal is this module's own diagnostic, and a caller that
// builds a body past the bound gets it.
//   parameter_cap 8       372 reason 2.  Refuses at 9, accepts 8 and 7.
//   walk_depth_cap 64     375 reason 9.  Refuses past 64, accepts below.
//                          THE REFUSAL NOW NAMES ITS OWN QUANTITY: out[10] is
//                          the walk depth that crossed the cap and out[11] is
//                          64, both in WALK DEPTH and in neither nesting
//                          level nor env.depth_peak. Before this change the
//                          four words were a position and a family and a
//                          caller could not tell whether the cap was crossed
//                          at 65 or at 200 -- which was not a terse message,
//                          it was a quantity with no channel to travel in.
//                          FOUR SITES, AND THE FOURTH ANSWERED 381/1, NOT
//                          375/9. Three of the four sites raise this bound's
//                          own diagnostic. declares_below is a predicate and
//                          cannot raise a code, so its callers raise 381/1 --
//                          "the caller declares this name" -- for a cap
//                          crossing, with out[10]/out[11] left 0. That made a
//                          cap crossing byte-identical on the wire to a real
//                          collision, and it made this row's sentence "a
//                          refusal that is NOT a bound publishes 0 and 0"
//                          false at one of the four sites bound to this cap.
//                          It now writes the two words under note_bound, so
//                          the fourth site is distinguishable from the other
//                          three ONLY by its code and from a real collision
//                          by its quantity. See declares_below.
//   loop_cap 512          380 reason 2.  Measured under the frozen maxima:
//                          512 items expand, 513 answer 380/2. Before this
//                          change the measured ceiling was 177 items, and it
//                          was the host buffer table, not this cap.
//   template_item_cap 512 380 reason 2, per quoted block/generated function body.
//                          Both forms are measured at 511/512/513/1026 items.
//                          The private C bootstrap uses one aggregate template
//                          table per macro; that narrower compatibility limit
//                          is distinct from this declared per-body bound.
//   hard_name_cap 4194304 900 reason 1, in reserve_names. Reachable, and only
//                          on a body that writes LONG introduced names: a
//                          512x512 nested meta-for with one character names
//                          answers 201/3 (arena bytes) and the same shape with
//                          two hundred character names answers 900/1. That is
//                          the whole argument against writing a measured
//                          ceiling down as a constant: it is a property of
//                          the body, and the same loop counts answer a
//                          different bound when the names are longer.
//
// ENFORCED BUT UNREACHABLE, and the reason is arithmetic, so no body reaches
// it either:
//   hard_node_cap 262144  900 reason 1 in reserve_nodes, but charge_arena
//                          always answers first. node_bytes() is 96 and
//                          max_arena() is frozen at 16777216, and a comptime
//                          block may only LOWER a budget, so arena_bytes
//                          refuses at 174762 nodes -- below the 262144 at
//                          which reserve_nodes would refuse, for every body
//                          that exists. Measured: 512x512 nested meta-fors,
//                          262144 items, answers 201 reason 3. This cap is
//                          dead code. It is kept because it is a declared
//                          bound of the reference and removing it is a
//                          decision this module does not take; it is NOT a
//                          guarantee, and nothing should rely on it.
//   argument stack 4096   900 reason 1 in push_arg, but 4096 words is 1024
//                          chain levels and max_expansion_depth() is 128, so
//                          the depth budget answers 201 reason 5 at 129
//                          levels, eight times below. Measured.
//   binding stack 65536   900 reason 1 in push_binding. bind_stride is 7, so
//                          9362 entries is 65534 words, the largest count
//                          that fits, and the 9363rd entry is the one refused.
//                          A body that pushes 9363 of them holds 9363
//                          statements, and template_item_cap refuses that at
//                          512 -- eighteen times earlier. The entry this one
//                          replaces said "it needs 9362 lets of 7 words. A
//                          1200 let body still expands", and BOTH halves of
//                          that were wrong: a 1200 statement body is refused
//                          380 reason 2, and while the claim stood the 1200
//                          let body expanded TRUNCATED, in silence, as a
//                          success.
//
// The SIX declared budgets (the six fn above) are enforced by charge_* and
// answer 201 with the reason, and that is independent of everything here. The
// gate re-measures every ceiling on four body shapes, in
// compiler/c/nova/tests_ntasm_macro_expand.c.
//
// A compiler bound independent of the six budgets, so a declared budget can
// never make this module allocate without limit. See the unreachable entry
// above for what it does and does not bound.
fn hard_node_cap()->u64 { return 262144; }
// The name table's own ceiling: a bound on want*8, doubling from 64 words.
// See the reachable entry above for the shape it needs.
fn hard_name_cap()->u64 { return 4194304; }
// A meta-for may not project more than this many template items.
fn loop_cap()->u64 { return 512; }
// Nesting bound of the template walk, a compiler bound like the parser's.
fn walk_depth_cap()->u64 { return 64; }
// Macro parameter count, the reference value is 8.
fn parameter_cap()->u64 { return 8; }
// The reference's template table: how many top level items ONE macro body may
// hold, whatever its form. It is a DIFFERENT quantity from loop_cap above --
// that one is how many times a meta-for repeats, this one is how big the body
// it repeats is -- and the two are declared apart and measured apart on
// purpose. See template_items for what the reference does when it is passed.
fn template_item_cap()->u64 { return 512; }

// ------------------------------------------------------------- node kinds
fn k_const()->u64 { return 10; }
fn k_comptime()->u64 { return 11; }
fn k_ct_limit()->u64 { return 12; }
fn k_macro()->u64 { return 13; }
fn k_mparam()->u64 { return 14; }
fn k_fn()->u64 { return 18; }
fn k_param()->u64 { return 19; }
fn k_effects()->u64 { return 20; }
fn k_clobbers()->u64 { return 21; }
fn k_abi()->u64 { return 28; }
fn k_requires()->u64 { return 22; }
fn k_ensures()->u64 { return 23; }
fn k_data()->u64 { return 24; }
fn k_block()->u64 { return 25; }
fn k_let()->u64 { return 27; }
fn k_call()->u64 { return 28; }
fn k_return()->u64 { return 29; }
fn k_if()->u64 { return 30; }
fn k_expand()->u64 { return 31; }
fn k_insn()->u64 { return 32; }
fn k_memory()->u64 { return 34; }
fn k_expr()->u64 { return 35; }
fn k_type()->u64 { return 36; }
fn k_meta_block()->u64 { return 37; }
fn k_meta_let()->u64 { return 38; }
fn k_meta_if()->u64 { return 39; }
fn k_meta_for()->u64 { return 40; }
fn k_meta_return()->u64 { return 41; }
fn k_meta_emit()->u64 { return 42; }
fn k_quote()->u64 { return 43; }
fn k_name()->u64 { return 90; }
fn k_qualified()->u64 { return 91; }
fn k_dashed()->u64 { return 92; }
fn k_section_name()->u64 { return 93; }
fn k_literal()->u64 { return 94; }

// The parser gives the same node kind to an abi annotation and to a call
// statement. An abi node holds one dashed child, a call node always starts
// with a qualified callee, so the first child tells them apart.
fn is_abi(env:Env,id:u64)->bool {
    if nkind(env,id)!=k_abi() { return false; }
    if child_count(env,id)!=1 { return false; }
    return nkind(env,nth(env,id,0))==k_dashed();
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------ diagnostics
// 201 budget refusal, reason 1 fuel, 2 call depth, 3 arena bytes, 4 produced
// AST nodes, 5 expansion depth, 6 constant data bytes
// 203 the caller output buffer cannot hold the produced region
// 370 unknown macro at the invocation
// 371 macro arity mismatch
// 372 macro parameter kind, value or shape mismatch
// 373 a comptime value is used with no binding in scope
// 374 capture of a caller symbol
// 375 a template node kind outside the closed vocabulary
// 376 a template name shadows a macro parameter or a comptime binding, or is
//     one of the reserved words of word_id
// 377 macro form outside this profile
// 378 the comptime limit block is malformed
// 379 a meta-if or meta-for condition is not a deterministic constant. Its
//     reason is the evaluator status, and ABOVE that range four reasons name
//     WHICH forbidden channel a comptime expression reached for: 20 file read,
//     21 file write, 22 file handle close, 23 diagnostic output. A fifth family
//     -- clock, random, environment, subprocess, network -- has no primitive in
//     the Nova environment at all, so there is no channel to name and those
//     keep the evaluator's own reason. See channel_id.
// 380 a template's projected statement count is too large: reason 1 the
//     meta-for count is negative, reason 2 either it is past loop_cap or the
//     body it repeats holds more than template_item_cap items
// 381 a generated symbol collides with an existing caller symbol
// 386 expansion in a type position is not implemented in this profile
// 800 structural failure, nothing written
// 900 allocation failure
fn budget_fuel()->u64 { return 1; }
fn budget_call()->u64 { return 2; }
fn budget_arena()->u64 { return 3; }
fn budget_nodes()->u64 { return 4; }
fn budget_depth()->u64 { return 5; }
fn budget_constant()->u64 { return 6; }

// One produced node costs twelve words, so its arena cost is fixed.
fn node_bytes()->u64 { return 96; }
// Binding kinds in the binding stack.
fn bind_value()->u64 { return 0; }
fn bind_local()->u64 { return 1; }
fn bind_decl()->u64 { return 2; }
// Name positions: a binding position must resolve inside the template, a
// spelled position is copied verbatim.
fn pos_bind()->u64 { return 0; }
fn pos_spelled()->u64 { return 1; }
// A spliced caller argument is copied verbatim: its names belong to the
// caller scope, so they are neither resolved against the template bindings
// nor renamed, and a symbol position inside it is not a capture.
fn pos_caller()->u64 { return 2; }
// Macro result kinds.
fn result_expr()->u64 { return 1; }
fn result_stmt()->u64 { return 2; }
fn result_decl()->u64 { return 3; }
fn result_type()->u64 { return 4; }
fn result_block()->u64 { return 5; }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- the state
// Every mutable field lives in this record, which is passed by reference to
// every helper, so the whole expansion shares one budget account.
struct Env {
    source:buf<u8>,tree:ntasm_ast.Tree,
    fuel_limit:u64,call_limit:u64,arena_limit:u64,
    node_limit:u64,depth_limit:u64,constant_limit:u64,
    fuel:u64,call_depth:u64,call_peak:u64,arena:u64,
    node_count:u64,depth:u64,depth_peak:u64,constant:u64,
    serial:u64,expansions:u64,macros:u64,nodes_made:u64,
    code:u64,reason:u64,line:u64,column:u64,
    // offending and limit are the QUANTITY a bound refused on and the cap it
    // refused against. They are not part of fail()'s signature: fail() is
    // called from 113 places, most of which are not a bound at all (a dead
    // id, a wrong node kind, a literal outside its range). Widening it would
    // put a fifth argument on all 113 to fill in at sixteen. Instead a bound
    // site writes these two and then calls refuse_bound below, and a refusal
    // that is NOT a bound leaves both at zero, which is how a caller tells
    // "refused 372 reason 2, on 9 macro parameters against a cap of 8" from
    // "refused 800 reason 1, on no quantity at all". SIXTEEN sites write them:
    // the six declared budgets, the six `fn *cap()` bounds, template_item_cap,
    // and the two literal capacities in push_binding and push_arg.
    offending:u64,limit:u64,
    node_words:buf<u64>,node_cap:u64,high:u64,scratch:bool,scratch_used:u64,
    // Set only around the clone of an expression the expander is about to
    // fold. resolve_name reads it, so a name spelled in a fold position is
    // classified as a channel attempt and a name spelled anywhere else keeps
    // the hygiene refusal. One bool, three writers, one reader.
    folding:bool,
    name_words:buf<u64>,name_cap:u64,name_bytes:u64,
    arg:buf<u64>,arg_cap:u64,arg_top:u64,
    // bind is one stack for the whole expansion. scope_base is the word
    // offset a nested expansion starts its own bindings above, so a lookup
    // never reaches the entries of the expansion that opened it and a push
    // never overwrites them.
    bind:buf<u64>,bind_cap:u64,bind_top:u64,scope_base:u64,
    frames:buf<u64>,dummy:buf<u64>
}

fn fresh_env(source:buf<u8>,tree:ntasm_ast.Tree)->Env {
    let none:buf<u64>=alloc(runtime(),0);
    let nodes:buf<u64>=alloc(runtime(),0);
    let names:buf<u64>=alloc(runtime(),0);
    let args:buf<u64>=alloc(runtime(),0);
    let binds:buf<u64>=alloc(runtime(),0);
    let frames:buf<u64>=alloc(runtime(),0);
    return Env{source:source,tree:tree,
        fuel_limit:max_fuel(),call_limit:max_call_depth(),arena_limit:max_arena(),
        node_limit:max_ast_nodes(),depth_limit:max_expansion_depth(),
        constant_limit:max_constant_bytes(),
        fuel:0,call_depth:0,call_peak:0,arena:0,node_count:0,depth:0,
        depth_peak:0,constant:0,serial:0,expansions:0,macros:0,nodes_made:0,
        code:0,reason:0,line:0,column:0,
        offending:0,limit:0,
        node_words:nodes,node_cap:0,high:0,scratch:false,scratch_used:0,folding:false,name_words:names,name_cap:0,name_bytes:0,
        arg:args,arg_cap:0,arg_top:0,bind:binds,bind_cap:0,bind_top:0,scope_base:0,
        frames:frames,dummy:none};
}

fn release_env(env:Env)->u64 {
    let nodes:buf<u64>=env.node_words;
    let names:buf<u64>=env.name_words;
    let args:buf<u64>=env.arg;
    let binds:buf<u64>=env.bind;
    let none:buf<u64>=env.dummy;
    let frames:buf<u64>=env.frames;
    rt_free(runtime(),nodes);rt_free(runtime(),names);
    rt_free(runtime(),args);rt_free(runtime(),binds);rt_free(runtime(),frames);rt_free(runtime(),none);
    return 0;
}

// First failure wins, so the diagnostic is stable.
fn fail(env:Env,code:u64,reason:u64,line:u64,column:u64)->u64 {
    if env.code==0 {
        env.code=code;env.reason=reason;env.line=line;env.column=column;
    }
    return 1;
}

// fail() plus the two words that say WHICH quantity crossed WHICH cap. The
// guard is fail()'s, byte for byte, and it has to be: if a bound site wrote
// offending after some earlier failure had already claimed env.code, the
// refusal a caller reads would carry the quantity of a bound that never
// answered. First failure wins, and so does its quantity.
fn refuse_bound(env:Env,code:u64,reason:u64,line:u64,column:u64,
                offending:u64,limit:u64)->u64 {
    if env.code==0 { env.offending=offending;env.limit=limit; }
    return fail(env,code,reason,line,column);
}

// For the two reserve_* sites, which answer 1 to their CALLER and let the
// caller raise the code. The two words are written under the same guard and
// then the caller fails as it always did, so the refusal a caller reads keeps
// the code it had and gains the quantity. Writing them here is safe precisely
// because guard and caller are adjacent: nothing runs between the write and
// the fail.
fn note_bound(env:Env,offending:u64,limit:u64)->u64 {
    if env.code==0 { env.offending=offending;env.limit=limit; }
    return 0;
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------ tree reading
//
// WHY THERE IS NO node_at HERE, and it is the reason this module's declared
// bounds used to mean nothing. ntasm_ast.node_at returns a record by value
// across a module boundary, and nova.c lowers a record literal to NV2_ALLOC:
// every single call is one live host buffer, 96 bytes for a node, that this
// bootstrap never reclaims. The handle of a record used in an expression has
// no name, so there is nothing for rt_free to name either. A walk that reads
// a node per step therefore spends one buffer table slot per step, and the
// table is BUFFER_MAX = 65536 (runtime_v2.c:9).
//
// Measured, in a proven-cold build directory (out/c-bootstrap/nova/
// w14-buffer-base-a91f3c for the before, the instrument
// out/c-bootstrap/nova/w14-probe-b77e01 for all three arms). Across the body
// shapes swept, add_buffer was called 4502073 times and the overwhelming
// majority were length 96: a 177 item meta-for, under a third of loop_cap,
// cost 60277 allocations on its own, and a 16x32 nested pair cost more than
// the whole table. The table filled, the host raised an error, and the
// expander returned 0 with out[0] still 0: a refusal the expander never
// made, published as a success. That ceiling, not loop_cap 512 and not
// hard_node_cap 262144, was the bound five of this module's seven declared
// bounds were measured against.
//
// The three arms, same probe, same directory, so the two allocations this
// change removed are separable and each removal is shown to matter:
//   before, both leaks present          4502073 calls, peak live 65536
//   node reads fixed, literals still    1813735 calls, peak live 65536
//   both fixed                             8952 calls, peak live    436
// The second of those is text_is: a `let t:buf<u8>="fuel"` is one buffer per
// spelling tested, and word_id tests a spelling for every name it meets. It is
// the same defect, a different allocation, and it only became visible once
// the first one was gone.
//
// So the words are read out of tree.node_words here, which allocates nothing,
// which is the form ntir.nova:98-104 and stub_table.nova:9-13 already use and
// the form the note this comment replaces had already asked for. A node id
// crosses a function boundary as a u64 and a field is read where it is used.
// ast.nova still exports node_at: that is another module's choice, and this
// module no longer depends on it.
fn live(env:Env,id:u64)->bool {
    return id!=0 && id<=env.tree.node_count;
}

// Word `field` of the caller tree node `id`. A node that is not live answers
// zero, which is exactly what the all-zero record this replaced answered, so
// no caller's arithmetic changes and the out of range case stays the one
// answer it was.
fn nword(env:Env,id:u64,field:u64)->u64 {
    if live(env,id)==false { return 0; }
    let n:buf<u64>=env.tree.node_words;
    return n[(id-1)*12+field];
}

fn nkind(env:Env,id:u64)->u64 { return nword(env,id,0); }
fn ntoken_begin(env:Env,id:u64)->u64 { return nword(env,id,1); }
fn ntoken_end(env:Env,id:u64)->u64 { return nword(env,id,2); }
fn nsource(env:Env,id:u64)->u64 { return nword(env,id,3); }
fn nline(env:Env,id:u64)->u64 { return nword(env,id,4); }
fn ncolumn(env:Env,id:u64)->u64 { return nword(env,id,5); }
fn nstart(env:Env,id:u64)->u64 { return nword(env,id,6); }
fn nend(env:Env,id:u64)->u64 { return nword(env,id,7); }
fn nfirst_child(env:Env,id:u64)->u64 { return nword(env,id,8); }
fn nlast_child(env:Env,id:u64)->u64 { return nword(env,id,9); }
fn nnext_sibling(env:Env,id:u64)->u64 { return nword(env,id,10); }
fn naux(env:Env,id:u64)->u64 { return nword(env,id,11); }

fn kid(env:Env,id:u64)->u64 { return nfirst_child(env,id); }
fn sib(env:Env,id:u64)->u64 { return nnext_sibling(env,id); }

// The nth child of a node, zero based.
fn nth(env:Env,id:u64,index:u64)->u64 {
    let mut at_id:u64=kid(env,id);
    let mut seen:u64=0;
    while at_id!=0 && seen<index {
        if live(env,at_id)==false { return 0; }
        at_id=nnext_sibling(env,at_id);seen=seen+1;
    }
    if live(env,at_id)==false { return 0; }
    return at_id;
}

// WHY THERE IS NO WALK BOUND HERE, because there was one and it was the worst
// defect this module has carried.
//
// child_count used to walk siblings under `seen<=1024` and RETURN THE COUNT IT
// REACHED. A quoted block body is walked by index from its quote node, whose
// first child is its kind word, so a body of 1025 statements was expanded as
// if it had 1024 and the result was PUBLISHED: code 0, runtime 0, a complete
// looking node region, and no diagnostic anywhere. A caller could not tell a
// truncated expansion from a whole one, and because it was a SUCCESS nothing
// downstream was protected by anything either. Measured on one body shape,
// one fuel budget and one binary:
//
//   1024 lets, 1025 items    5122 published nodes, code 0, last let PRESENT
//   1025 lets, 1026 items    5122 published nodes, code 0, last let ABSENT
//   9363 lets, 9364 items    5122 published nodes, code 0, last let ABSENT
//
// A silent truncation reported as a success is the shape this project's own
// rule is about -- an instrument that cannot see the case it exists to detect
// -- with no red anywhere, because the truncation IS the answer rather than a
// failed check.
//
// So the count is the count. The walk runs to the end of the chain on the two
// conditions it always used, a zero terminator and a dead node, and the trust
// model is the one run_block, declares_below and walk have always relied on:
// the parser owns chain wellformedness, and ntir.nova:869-872 asserts it, a
// chain holding exactly child_count nodes whose last one ends it.
//
// A body too big for this module to expand is no longer a SMALLER body. It is
// template_items below, which refuses it with a code and a position, at the
// reference's own 512.
fn child_count(env:Env,id:u64)->u64 {
    let mut at_id:u64=kid(env,id);
    let mut seen:u64=0;
    while at_id!=0 {
        if live(env,at_id)==false { break; }
        seen=seen+1;at_id=nnext_sibling(env,at_id);
    }
    return seen;
}

// THE ITEM COUNT OF A MACRO BODY'S OWN TOP LEVEL LIST, refused past the
// reference's template table. This is the answer the walk above used to
// invent, and it is the REFERENCE's answer rather than this module's.
//
// WHAT THE REFERENCE DOES, measured rather than read off the source. A macro
// body that returns a quoted block holds its statements in MacroTemplate
// templates[512] (frontend.c:57) and frontend.c:3782 refuses the 513th with
// NTF_E_LIMIT, 201, "quoted block statement limit exceeded", at p->token.span,
// which is the position of the 513th statement itself. Run against the C
// reference on this body shape:
//
//   511 statements   compiles, verified, 514 statements published
//   512 statements   refused 201 at the 513th item, nothing published
//   1025 statements  refused 201 at the 513th item, nothing published
//   2048 statements  refused 201 at the 513th item, nothing published
//
// So the reference ACCEPTS 512 items and REFUSES THE 513TH LOUDLY. Nova
// accepted 1024 statements and dropped the rest in silence: wider than the
// reference by a factor of two, and quieter than it in the half that
// matters. Refusing here is therefore not a bound Nova invents and not a
// narrowing Nova imposes on a shape the reference expands -- it is the
// reference's own bound at the reference's own value, and the range Nova
// gives up is exactly the range the reference never expanded either.
//
// WHY 380 AND NOT A NEW CODE. 380 is "the template's projected statement
// count is too large", and the reference answers NTF_E_LIMIT for BOTH of its
// 512s -- the meta-for repeat count at frontend.c:2241 and the template table
// at frontend.c:3782 -- so one code carries both the way one code carries
// both there. The two quantities are not the same number and are never
// reported as the same: loop_cap() is the repeat count, template_item_cap()
// is the size of the body being repeated, and the gate measures them apart.
//
// ONE RESIDUAL, named rather than hidden. The reference's 512 is ONE table
// shared by every template item of the macro, so a body at exactly 512 with
// other template items beside it is refused there and is one count wider
// here, because Nova's meta-block items and a quote's statements are two
// lists and the reference has one. Every shape the gate builds agrees.
//
// `skip` is the index of the first item in the child chain: 0 when the
// children are all items, 1 for a quote whose first child is its kind word.
// On refusal the position is the first item PAST the cap, the token the
// reference reports on. The return value is the count and nothing else: on
// refusal this returns 0 AND sets env.code, so a caller must test env.code
// and never the value, because an empty body is a legal count of zero.
fn template_items(env:Env,id:u64,skip:u64)->u64 {
    let count:u64=child_count(env,id);
    if count>skip+template_item_cap() {
        let over:u64=nth(env,id,skip+template_item_cap());
        let mut at:u64=over;
        if live(env,over)==false { at=id; }
        refuse_bound(env,380,2,nline(env,at),ncolumn(env,at),
                     count-skip,template_item_cap());
        return 0;
    }
    return count;
}

// ---- the token a materialised literal carries -----------------------------
// A literal built by emit_constant has no source text of its own: it was
// folded from a comptime value, not written. The node still has to carry a
// token, because ntir.type_literal classifies a literal BY its token and
// refuses anything that is not an integer or a string token. The token array
// belongs to the caller's lexer -- produced() hands this module no tokens of
// its own -- so a token cannot be minted here, and asking the IR to accept a
// token kind the lexer never produces would put this module's convention into
// the contract of the thing the product is being held to.
//
// The token is therefore the caller's own: the first INTEGER token inside the
// range the constant was folded from, and that range is the folded
// expression's own token span, so the token is always one that contributed to
// the value and never one borrowed from an unrelated line. A range holding no
// integer token -- a comptime value bound from a boolean word -- has none, and
// the node keeps the template position token it has always carried.
//
// The two node words are read through the shared reader, which is the same
// direct word read this note always asked for -- it just is not two readers
// of its own any more, because nothing in this module reads a node as a
// record any more. The reason is written down at the top of the tree reading
// block and it is not a style preference: it was the ceiling behind five of
// the seven declared bounds.
fn fold_token_begin(env:Env,id:u64)->u64 { return ntoken_begin(env,id); }
fn fold_token_end(env:Env,id:u64)->u64 { return ntoken_end(env,id); }
// The lexer's integer token kind (lexer.nova:117), written out for the same
// reason ntir.nova:117-118 writes it: the token kinds are the lexer's numbers
// and no module exports them.
fn tok_int()->u64 { return 258; }

// The index of the first integer token in [from,to], BIASED BY ONE, so zero
// can mean "this range holds no integer token" without colliding with token
// zero. Token zero is a real index -- it is the first token of every stream --
// so an unbiased answer would let a module whose first token is an integer
// read as "no token found", which is the guard depending on the value it is
// checking.
fn first_int_token(env:Env,from:u64,to:u64)->u64 {
    let tokens:buf<u64>=env.tree.token_words;
    if from>to || to>=env.tree.token_count { return 0; }
    let mut i:u64=from;
    while i<=to {
        if tokens[i*9]==tok_int() { return i+1; }
        i=i+1;
    }
    return 0;
}

// The token a materialised literal of the value folded from source_id carries,
// biased by one as first_int_token returns it, and 0 when the folded range
// named no integer token.
fn folded_token(env:Env,source_id:u64)->u64 {
    if live(env,source_id)==false { return 0; }
    return first_int_token(env,fold_token_begin(env,source_id),
        fold_token_end(env,source_id));
}

// Rewrite the two token words of a produced literal. A zero bias leaves the
// node exactly as emit stamped it, which is the only correct answer when the
// folded range held no integer token to name.
fn stamp_literal_token(env:Env,id:u64,bias:u64)->u64 {
    if bias==0 || prod_live(env,id)==false { return 0; }
    let token:u64=bias-1;
    let words:buf<u64>=env.node_words;
    let w:u64=(id-1)*12;
    words[w+1]=token;words[w+2]=token;return 0;
}

// A spelled name is compared against a byte count and up to three packed u64
// words, never against a buffer. A `let t:buf<u8>="fuel"` is one host buffer
// allocation, and this module makes one per name it tests, so comparing
// against a buffer literal is the SECOND form of the same leak -- the one
// that survived removing the record node reads, and the one that then became
// the binding ceiling. The packing is stated once, here: byte i of the text
// is bit (i%8)*8 of word i/8, and the three words are little endian in that
// order, so a text of up to 24 bytes is three u64 literals.
fn text_is(env:Env,start:u64,end:u64,n:u64,w0:u64,w1:u64,w2:u64)->bool {
    let source:buf<u8>=env.source;
    if n>24 { return false; }
    if start>end || end>len(source) { return false; }
    if (end-start)!=n { return false; }
    let mut i:u64=0;
    while i<n {
        let mut word:u64=w0;
        if i>=8 { word=w1; }
        if i>=16 { word=w2; }
        if source[start+i]!=((word>>((i%8)*8))&255) { return false; }
        i=i+1;
    }
    return true;
}

// Two source spans carry the same identifier when their texts are equal.
// Positions are not identity: a declaration and its reference are two
// occurrences, so a hygiene lookup must compare the spelling, never the span.
fn same_text(env:Env,astart:u64,aend:u64,bstart:u64,bend:u64)->bool {
    let source:buf<u8>=env.source;
    if aend-astart!=bend-bstart { return false; }
    if astart>aend || bstart>bend { return false; }
    if aend>len(source) || bend>len(source) { return false; }
    let mut i:u64=0;
    while i<(aend-astart) {
        if source[astart+i]!=source[bstart+i] { return false; }
        i=i+1;
    }
    return true;
}

fn named(env:Env,id:u64,n:u64,w0:u64,w1:u64,w2:u64)->bool {
    if live(env,id)==false { return false; }
    let node:u64=id;
    if nkind(env,node)!=k_name() { return false; }
    return text_is(env,nstart(env,node),nend(env,node),n,w0,w1,w2);
}

// A Nova buffer parameter must name a variable, so every spelling the
// expander compares against is bound once here and selected by a small
// number. The set is closed: 1..6 the six comptime limit names, 7..11 the
// five ast result and parameter kinds, 12..20 the nine scalar type names,
// 21 and 22 the two boolean words.
fn word_id(env:Env,id:u64,which:u64)->bool {
    if which==1 { return named(env,id,4,0x000000006c657566,0x0000000000000000,0x0000000000000000); }
    if which==2 { return named(env,id,10,0x7065645f6c6c6163,0x0000000000006874,0x0000000000000000); }
    if which==3 { return named(env,id,11,0x79625f616e657261,0x0000000000736574,0x0000000000000000); }
    if which==4 { return named(env,id,9,0x65646f6e5f747361,0x0000000000000073,0x0000000000000000); }
    if which==5 { return named(env,id,15,0x6f69736e61707865,0x0068747065645f6e,0x0000000000000000); }
    if which==6 { return named(env,id,14,0x746e6174736e6f63,0x000073657479625f,0x0000000000000000); }
    if which==7 { return named(env,id,4,0x0000000072707865,0x0000000000000000,0x0000000000000000); }
    if which==8 { return named(env,id,4,0x0000000065707974,0x0000000000000000,0x0000000000000000); }
    if which==9 { return named(env,id,4,0x00000000746d7473,0x0000000000000000,0x0000000000000000); }
    if which==10 { return named(env,id,4,0x000000006c636564,0x0000000000000000,0x0000000000000000); }
    if which==11 { return named(env,id,5,0x0000006b636f6c62,0x0000000000000000,0x0000000000000000); }
    if which==12 { return named(env,id,4,0x000000006c6f6f62,0x0000000000000000,0x0000000000000000); }
    if which==13 { return named(env,id,2,0x0000000000003875,0x0000000000000000,0x0000000000000000); }
    if which==14 { return named(env,id,3,0x0000000000363175,0x0000000000000000,0x0000000000000000); }
    if which==15 { return named(env,id,3,0x0000000000323375,0x0000000000000000,0x0000000000000000); }
    if which==16 { return named(env,id,3,0x0000000000343675,0x0000000000000000,0x0000000000000000); }
    if which==17 { return named(env,id,2,0x0000000000003869,0x0000000000000000,0x0000000000000000); }
    if which==18 { return named(env,id,3,0x0000000000363169,0x0000000000000000,0x0000000000000000); }
    if which==19 { return named(env,id,3,0x0000000000323369,0x0000000000000000,0x0000000000000000); }
    if which==20 { return named(env,id,3,0x0000000000343669,0x0000000000000000,0x0000000000000000); }
    if which==21 { return named(env,id,4,0x0000000065757274,0x0000000000000000,0x0000000000000000); }
    if which==22 { return named(env,id,5,0x00000065736c6166,0x0000000000000000,0x0000000000000000); }
    return false;
}

// The closed reserved set. word_id already compares a node against each of
// the twenty two words the module and the expander read as text, so this is
// that same set under one predicate: the six comptime limit names, the five
// ast result and parameter kinds, the nine scalar type names and the two
// boolean literals. A template that binds one of them makes the word mean
// two things, and the boolean literals are already load bearing: run_meta_if
// decides a branch from the word, so a template could otherwise bind true to
// zero and still take the then branch. Refusing the whole set is the
// auditable form of the rule, and it is the shape 376 already has for a
// template name that shadows a binding.
fn reserved_word(env:Env,id:u64)->bool {
    let mut which:u64=1;
    while which<=22 {
        if word_id(env,id,which) { return true; }
        which=which+1;
    }
    return false;
}

// ------------------------------------------------------- produced storage
fn reserve_nodes(env:Env,extra:u64)->u64 {
    if env.high+extra<=env.node_cap { return 0; }
    let mut want:u64=env.node_cap;
    if want==0 { want=64; }
    while want<(env.high+extra) { want=want*2; }
    if want>hard_node_cap() { note_bound(env,want,hard_node_cap());return 1; }
    let words:buf<u64>=alloc(runtime(),want*12);
    if len(words)!=want*12 { return 1; }
    let old:buf<u64>=env.node_words;
    let mut i:u64=0;
    let keep:u64=env.node_cap*12;
    while i<keep { words[i]=old[i];i=i+1; }
    env.node_words=words;env.node_cap=want;
    rt_free(runtime(),old);
    return 0;
}

fn reserve_names(env:Env,extra_bytes:u64)->u64 {
    if env.name_bytes+extra_bytes<=(env.name_cap*8) { return 0; }
    let mut want:u64=env.name_cap;
    if want==0 { want=64; }
    while want*8<(env.name_bytes+extra_bytes) { want=want*2; }
    if want*8>hard_name_cap() { note_bound(env,want*8,hard_name_cap());return 1; }
    let words:buf<u64>=alloc(runtime(),want);
    if len(words)!=want { return 1; }
    let old:buf<u64>=env.name_words;
    let mut i:u64=0;
    let keep:u64=env.name_cap;
    while i<keep { words[i]=old[i];i=i+1; }
    env.name_words=words;env.name_cap=want;
    rt_free(runtime(),old);
    return 0;
}

// The produced region is itself a valid node_words buffer, so the only
// compile time evaluator can read an expansion result without any new
// evaluator being written. The high water mark is published, never the
// scratch high water of a temporary, so a folded condition cannot be read as
// part of the result.
//
// This is the ONE record this module still builds, and it is the whole of
// what is left: a Tree is twelve words, so a fold costs 96 bytes, and
// constant_eval.evaluate builds its own Value record beside it. Removing
// them needs a change to constant_eval.nova, which takes the Tree by value
// and is not this module's file, so the number is declared here rather than
// hidden. It is two allocations per folded constant -- see the tree reading
// block for what the other 2271856 of them used to be.
fn produced(env:Env)->ntasm_ast.Tree {
    return ntasm_ast.Tree{ok:true,token_words:env.dummy,token_count:0,
        node_words:env.node_words,node_count:env.high,
        code:0,reason:0,source:0,line:0,column:0,start:0,end:0};
}

fn prod_live(env:Env,id:u64)->bool { return id!=0 && id<=env.high; }

// prod_at used to sit here and was the produced-region twin of at(). It had
// no call site: every reader of the produced region goes through stamp_
// literal_token or the word loop in run()'s publish, both of which read
// env.node_words directly. It is removed rather than kept as an unused
// second way to spend a buffer, because an unused accessor is exactly the
// thing that starts being used again.

// ---------------------------------------------------------- budget charges
// Every charge is refused before it is applied, so reaching a limit is
// allowed and one step further is a stable error.
fn charge_fuel(env:Env,line:u64,column:u64)->u64 {
    if env.fuel>=env.fuel_limit {
        return refuse_bound(env,201,budget_fuel(),line,column,
                           env.fuel+1,env.fuel_limit);
    }
    env.fuel=env.fuel+1;return 0;
}

fn charge_arena(env:Env,bytes:u64,line:u64,column:u64)->u64 {
    if bytes>env.arena_limit-env.arena {
        return refuse_bound(env,201,budget_arena(),line,column,
                           env.arena+bytes,env.arena_limit);
    }
    env.arena=env.arena+bytes;return 0;
}

fn charge_constant(env:Env,bytes:u64,line:u64,column:u64)->u64 {
    if bytes>env.constant_limit-env.constant {
        return refuse_bound(env,201,budget_constant(),line,column,
                           env.constant+bytes,env.constant_limit);
    }
    env.constant=env.constant+bytes;return 0;
}

// Every charge is refused before it is applied, so reaching a limit is
// allowed and one step further is a stable error. nodes_made counts every node
// the expansion produced, including the temporaries of a folded condition,
// while node_count counts only the nodes the caller receives.
fn take_node(env:Env,line:u64,column:u64)->u64 {
    if env.nodes_made>=env.node_limit {
        refuse_bound(env,201,budget_nodes(),line,column,
                     env.nodes_made+1,env.node_limit);return 0;
    }
    if charge_arena(env,node_bytes(),line,column)!=0 { return 0; }
    if charge_fuel(env,line,column)!=0 { return 0; }
    env.nodes_made=env.nodes_made+1;
    let mut id:u64=0;
    if env.scratch==true {
        env.scratch_used=env.scratch_used+1;
        id=env.node_count+env.scratch_used;
    } else {
        env.node_count=env.node_count+1;id=env.node_count;
    }
    if id>env.high {
        if reserve_nodes(env,id-env.high)!=0 { fail(env,900,1,line,column);return 0; }
        env.high=id;
    }
    return id;
}

// A temporary region: the ids are reused by the next real node, the budgets
// are still charged, and nothing temporary is ever published.
fn scratch_begin(env:Env)->u64 {
    let saved:u64=env.node_count;
    env.scratch=true;env.scratch_used=0;return saved;
}

fn scratch_end(env:Env)->u64 {
    env.scratch=false;env.scratch_used=0;return 0;
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------ name storage
// Every produced name lives in the payload name table, so a consumer never
// has to look into a source buffer to read a symbol, generated or copied.
fn put_byte(env:Env,value:u64,line:u64,column:u64)->u64 {
    if reserve_names(env,1)!=0 { return fail(env,900,1,line,column); }
    let words:buf<u64>=env.name_words;
    let w:u64=env.name_bytes/8;
    let shift:u64=(env.name_bytes%8)*8;
    if env.name_bytes%8==0 { words[w]=value; }
    else { words[w]=words[w] | (value<<shift); }
    env.name_bytes=env.name_bytes+1;return 0;
}

fn put_text(env:Env,source:buf<u8>,start:u64,end:u64,line:u64,column:u64)->u64 {
    if start>end || end>len(source) { return fail(env,800,1,line,column); }
    if charge_constant(env,end-start,line,column)!=0 { return 1; }
    if reserve_names(env,end-start)!=0 { return fail(env,900,1,line,column); }
    let mut i:u64=start;
    while i<end {
        if put_byte(env,source[i],line,column)!=0 { return 1; }
        i=i+1;
    }
    return 0;
}

// The same three packed words, written out instead of compared. Charged and
// stored exactly as put_text stores a source range, so a mark fragment costs
// the same constant bytes and the same name table as it always did and
// nothing else.
fn put_words(env:Env,n:u64,w0:u64,w1:u64,w2:u64,line:u64,column:u64)->u64 {
    // The 24 is three packed words of eight bytes, and this refuses on the
    // BYTE COUNT, so it is a cap and not a shape check. It is the only
    // `fail()` in the module guarded by a numeric limit: the 201/900 capacity
    // paths publish through refuse_bound or note_bound, and every other
    // refusal is on a node's kind, existence or identity, where 0/0 is the
    // honest answer. Before wave 23 this one published nothing at all.
    if n>24 { return refuse_bound(env,800,2,line,column,n,24); }
    if charge_constant(env,n,line,column)!=0 { return 1; }
    if reserve_names(env,n)!=0 { return fail(env,900,1,line,column); }
    let mut i:u64=0;
    while i<n {
        let mut word:u64=w0;
        if i>=8 { word=w1; }
        if i>=16 { word=w2; }
        if put_byte(env,(word>>((i%8)*8))&255,line,column)!=0 { return 1; }
        i=i+1;
    }
    return 0;
}

fn put_number(env:Env,value:u64,line:u64,column:u64)->u64 {
    let mut digits:u64=0;
    let mut scale:u64=1;
    let mut rest:u64=value;
    while rest!=0 { digits=digits+1;scale=scale*10;rest=rest/10; }
    if digits==0 {
        if put_byte(env,48,line,column)!=0 { return 1; }
        return 0;
    }
    let mut place:u64=scale;
    while place>1 {
        place=place/10;
        let digit:u64=(value/place)%10;
        if put_byte(env,48+digit,line,column)!=0 { return 1; }
    }
    return 0;
}

// Produce a name node whose text is the byte range start..start+length of the
// name table. aux is 1 to tell a consumer that start and end address the name
// table and not a source buffer.
fn emit_name(env:Env,origin:u64,inv:u64,start:u64,
             length:u64)->u64 {
    let id:u64=take_node(env,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv));
    if id==0 { return 0; }
    let words:buf<u64>=env.node_words;
    let w:u64=(id-1)*12;
    words[w]=k_name();words[w+1]=ntoken_begin(env,origin);words[w+2]=ntoken_end(env,origin);
    words[w+3]=nsource(env,origin);words[w+4]=nline(env,inv);words[w+5]=ncolumn(env,inv);
    words[w+6]=start;words[w+7]=start+length;
    words[w+8]=0;words[w+9]=0;words[w+10]=0;words[w+11]=1;
    return id;
}

// Copy a spelled name into the name table and produce it.
fn emit_name_copy(env:Env,origin:u64,inv:u64)->u64 {
    let start:u64=env.name_bytes;
    if put_text(env,env.source,nstart(env,origin),nend(env,origin),nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 {
        return 0;
    }
    return emit_name(env,origin,inv,start,nend(env,origin)-nstart(env,origin));
}

// Produce a node. source, token_begin and token_end are the origin, that is
// the exact source and token range the shape was copied from: the macro
// template for a clone, the caller argument for a splice. line and column are
// the invocation position and start and end are the invocation byte span,
// with one exception below.
//
// A parameter (kind 19) is the one kind whose own span is load bearing. The
// parser records it on the direction keyword: node(p,19,ti(p)) takes the
// keyword token as the node begin, so nodes[at+6] is the first byte of "in",
// "out" or "inout", and finish leaves that start in place. The keyword is then
// stepped over and discarded, so it leaves no child and no other AST field
// carries the direction anywhere. abi.check (stage 17), calls.check (28) and
// call_types.check (29) all read the direction back from the source bytes at
// parameter.start, and none of those three readers can be replaced from here,
// so stamping the invocation span on a produced parameter destroys the only
// copy of the direction: the produced parameter reads the invocation text, the
// direction comes back 0, and the whole pipeline refuses the module with 800.
//
// The origin span restores exactly the two words the parser would have
// recorded, and the source buffer validate.nova hands to the later stages is
// the caller source with the expander name table appended after it, so the
// original bytes are still at the original offsets. Every other kind keeps
// the invocation span, which is the choice respan_types in validate.nova
// re-derives from a node's own children when a kind needs its own text.
fn emit(env:Env,kind:u64,auxv:u64,origin:u64,inv:u64,
        start:u64,end:u64)->u64 {
    let id:u64=take_node(env,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv));
    if id==0 { return 0; }
    let w:u64=(id-1)*12;
    env.node_words[w]=kind;env.node_words[w+1]=ntoken_begin(env,origin);
    env.node_words[w+2]=ntoken_end(env,origin);env.node_words[w+3]=nsource(env,origin);
    env.node_words[w+4]=nline(env,inv);env.node_words[w+5]=ncolumn(env,inv);
    if kind==k_param() {
        env.node_words[w+6]=nstart(env,origin);env.node_words[w+7]=nend(env,origin);
    } else {
        env.node_words[w+6]=start;env.node_words[w+7]=end;
    }
    env.node_words[w+8]=0;env.node_words[w+9]=0;env.node_words[w+10]=0;
    env.node_words[w+11]=auxv;
    return id;
}

fn prod_attach(env:Env,parent:u64,child:u64)->u64 {
    if child==0 { return 0; }
    if prod_live(env,parent)==false {
        return fail(env,900,99,parent,child);
    }
    let w:u64=(parent-1)*12;
    if env.node_words[w+8]==0 { env.node_words[w+8]=child; }
    else {
        let last:u64=env.node_words[w+9];
        if prod_live(env,last)==false { return 0; }
        env.node_words[(last-1)*12+10]=child;
    }
    env.node_words[w+9]=child;return child;
}

// --------------------------------------------------------- binding stack
// A binding is seven words: kind, negative, magnitude, fresh offset, fresh
// length, template name node, and the caller node the value was folded from. A
// value binding carries a folded constant and no template, a name binding
// carries the template name it was introduced under and the hygienic text it
// was rewritten to. The seventh word is the origin a materialised literal
// takes its token from, and it is 0 for every binding that is not a folded
// comptime value: only a folded value has to name a token. The newest match
// wins, so a later introduction shadows an earlier one inside one expansion.
fn bind_stride()->u64 { return 7; }

// A binding index is a word offset into the binding buffer, so an entry is
// addressed by its own offset and never by an entry number.
fn bind_field(env:Env,index:u64,field:u64)->u64 {
    let words:buf<u64>=env.bind;return words[index+field];
}

fn push_binding(env:Env,kind:u64,negative:bool,magnitude:u64,start:u64,
                length:u64,template:u64,origin:u64)->u64 {
    if env.bind_top+bind_stride()>env.bind_cap {
        let mut want:u64=env.bind_cap;
        if want==0 { want=256; }
        while want<(env.bind_top+bind_stride()) { want=want*2; }
        if want>65536 { return refuse_bound(env,900,1,0,0,want,65536); }
        let words:buf<u64>=alloc(runtime(),want);
        if len(words)!=want { return fail(env,900,1,0,0); }
        let old:buf<u64>=env.bind;
        let mut i:u64=0;
        while i<env.bind_cap { words[i]=old[i];i=i+1; }
        env.bind=words;env.bind_cap=want;rt_free(runtime(),old);
    }
    let w:u64=env.bind_top;
    let words:buf<u64>=env.bind;
    words[w]=kind;
    if negative { words[w+1]=1; } else { words[w+1]=0; }
    words[w+2]=magnitude;words[w+3]=start;words[w+4]=length;
    words[w+5]=template;words[w+6]=origin;
    env.bind_top=env.bind_top+bind_stride();return 0;
}

fn bind_kind(env:Env,index:u64)->u64 { return bind_field(env,index,0); }

fn bind_negative(env:Env,index:u64)->bool { return bind_field(env,index,1)==1; }

fn bind_magnitude(env:Env,index:u64)->u64 { return bind_field(env,index,2); }

fn bind_start(env:Env,index:u64)->u64 { return bind_field(env,index,3); }

fn bind_length(env:Env,index:u64)->u64 { return bind_field(env,index,4); }

fn bind_template(env:Env,index:u64)->u64 { return bind_field(env,index,5); }

// The caller node a folded value came from, 0 when the binding carries no
// folded value. It is read only by resolve_name, on the bind_value arm.
fn bind_origin(env:Env,index:u64)->u64 { return bind_field(env,index,6); }

// The word offset of the newest accepted binding for a template name, biased
// by one: the first binding of an expansion sits at offset zero, so zero
// cannot double as the not-found answer. The scan starts at scope_base, so a
// nested expansion never resolves a name against the expansion that opened
// it: that expansion is a different template scope and its marks differ, so
// matching its binding would be a capture and not a resolution.
fn bind_offset(env:Env,id:u64,accept_value:bool,accept_local:bool,
               accept_decl:bool)->u64 {
    if live(env,id)==false { return 0; }
    let name:u64=id;
    if nkind(env,name)!=k_name() { return 0; }
    let mut found:u64=0;
    let mut index:u64=env.scope_base;
    while index<env.bind_top {
        let template:u64=bind_template(env,index);
        if live(env,template)==true {
            let tn:u64=template;
            if same_text(env,nstart(env,tn),nend(env,tn),nstart(env,name),nend(env,name)) {
                let kind:u64=bind_kind(env,index);
                let ok:bool=(kind==bind_value()&&accept_value) ||
                    (kind==bind_local()&&accept_local) ||
                    (kind==bind_decl()&&accept_decl);
                if ok { found=index+1; }
            }
        }
        index=index+bind_stride();
    }
    return found;
}

fn binding_exists(env:Env,id:u64)->bool {
    if live(env,id)==false { return false; }
    let name:u64=id;
    let mut index:u64=env.scope_base;
    while index<env.bind_top {
        let template:u64=bind_template(env,index);
        if live(env,template)==true {
            let tn:u64=template;
            if same_text(env,nstart(env,tn),nend(env,tn),nstart(env,name),nend(env,name)) { return true; }
        }
        index=index+bind_stride();
    }
    return false;
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------ argument stack
// An argument is four words: tag, then either the caller node id, or the sign
// and the magnitude of a comptime value, and last the caller node the value
// was folded from. One shape for both keeps a splice from having to guess what
// the slot holds. The fourth word is 0 on the node arm, because a spliced
// caller node carries its own tokens and is never restamped; it is the
// argument node itself on the value arm, and it is what a materialised
// literal takes its token from.
fn arg_stride()->u64 { return 4; }
fn arg_node()->u64 { return 0; }
fn arg_value()->u64 { return 1; }
// A name the ENCLOSING template bound, carried as the fresh text's extent in
// the name table rather than as a caller node. The splice publishes those
// bytes verbatim, so the inner expansion refers to the outer's introduced
// name and not to whatever the caller happens to call it. See expand_node.
fn arg_mark()->u64 { return 2; }

fn push_node_arg(env:Env,node_id:u64)->u64 {
    return push_arg(env,arg_node(),node_id,0,0);
}

// An argument whose name the enclosing template introduced: the fresh text's
// start and length in the name table, and the source node it came from.
fn push_mark_arg(env:Env,start:u64,length:u64,origin:u64)->u64 {
    return push_arg(env,arg_mark(),start,length,origin);
}

// The name node of an argument that is nothing BUT a symbol reference, and 0
// for every other argument.
//
// AND THE SHAPE IS NOT THE OBVIOUS ONE, which cost two inert revisions of the
// push that consumes this. The argument of `expand inner(t)` is not a bare
// name: the parser builds a call's arguments as a QUALIFIED path, so `t` is a
// k_qualified holding exactly one k_name. The first revision tested the
// argument for k_name, the second also accepted k_expr, and both were silent
// and green on every nested expansion, because a branch nothing reaches does
// not fail -- it is simply never taken. The cut-every-site proof is what
// caught it, and it is why the helper accepts the three shapes by name here
// rather than by being widened at the call site until something matched.
//
// A path of one segment is the bare-reference form. `a+b` and a path of two or
// more segments both have more structure, and they are left to the scope that
// wrote them, which is where they belong.
fn argument_name(env:Env,argument:u64)->u64 {
    let n:u64=argument;
    // The EXPRESSION wrapper comes off first. The parser builds a call's
    // arguments as typed expressions, so `t` arrives as an expression holding
    // a one-segment path holding the name -- three layers, and each of the
    // first two revisions of the push tested only one of them and was
    // therefore inert on every nested expansion while the gate stayed green.
    if nkind(env,n)==k_expr() && naux(env,n)==0 && child_count(env,n)==1 {
        return argument_name(env,kid(env,n));
    }
    if nkind(env,n)==k_name() { return n; }
    if nkind(env,n)!=k_qualified() { return 0; }
    if child_count(env,n)!=1 { return 0; }
    let seg:u64=kid(env,n);
    if live(env,seg)==false { return 0; }
    if nkind(env,seg)!=k_name() { return 0; }
    return seg;
}

fn push_value_arg(env:Env,negative:bool,magnitude:u64,origin:u64)->u64 {
    let mut sign:u64=0;
    if negative { sign=1; }
    return push_arg(env,arg_value(),sign,magnitude,origin);
}

fn push_arg(env:Env,tag:u64,first:u64,second:u64,origin:u64)->u64 {
    if env.arg_top+arg_stride()>env.arg_cap {
        let mut want:u64=env.arg_cap;
        if want==0 { want=64; }
        while want<(env.arg_top+arg_stride()) { want=want*2; }
        if want>4096 { return refuse_bound(env,900,1,0,0,want,4096); }
        let words:buf<u64>=alloc(runtime(),want);
        if len(words)!=want { return fail(env,900,1,0,0); }
        let old:buf<u64>=env.arg;
        let mut i:u64=0;
        while i<env.arg_cap { words[i]=old[i];i=i+1; }
        env.arg=words;env.arg_cap=want;rt_free(runtime(),old);
    }
    let w:u64=env.arg_top;
    let words:buf<u64>=env.arg;
    words[w]=tag;words[w+1]=first;words[w+2]=second;words[w+3]=origin;
    env.arg_top=env.arg_top+arg_stride();return 0;
}

fn arg_tag(env:Env,index:u64)->u64 {
    let words:buf<u64>=env.arg;return words[index];
}

fn arg_first(env:Env,index:u64)->u64 {
    let words:buf<u64>=env.arg;return words[index+1];
}

fn arg_second(env:Env,index:u64)->u64 {
    let words:buf<u64>=env.arg;return words[index+2];
}

// The caller node a folded comptime value came from. 0 on the node arm, and 0
// never on the value arm, because the value arm is reached only after the
// argument node has been checked and a live id is in hand.
fn arg_origin(env:Env,index:u64)->u64 {
    let words:buf<u64>=env.arg;return words[index+3];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------- comptime block
// The six mandated limits, each verified before it is applied, each capped by
// the frozen maximum, no duplicate and none missing. A module with no comptime
// block runs on the frozen maxima, as frontend.c:5863-5868 does.
fn limit_index(env:Env,id:u64)->u64 {
    if word_id(env,id,1) { return 0; }
    if word_id(env,id,2) { return 1; }
    if word_id(env,id,3) { return 2; }
    if word_id(env,id,4) { return 3; }
    if word_id(env,id,5) { return 4; }
    if word_id(env,id,6) { return 5; }
    return 99;
}

fn limit_maximum(index:u64)->u64 {
    if index==0 { return max_fuel(); }
    if index==1 { return max_call_depth(); }
    if index==2 { return max_arena(); }
    if index==3 { return max_ast_nodes(); }
    if index==4 { return max_expansion_depth(); }
    return max_constant_bytes();
}

fn limit_store(env:Env,index:u64,value:u64)->u64 {
    if index==0 { env.fuel_limit=value;return 0; }
    if index==1 { env.call_limit=value;return 0; }
    if index==2 { env.arena_limit=value;return 0; }
    if index==3 { env.node_limit=value;return 0; }
    if index==4 { env.depth_limit=value;return 0; }
    env.constant_limit=value;return 0;
}

fn apply_comptime(env:Env,id:u64)->u64 {
    let mut values:buf<u64>=alloc(runtime(),6);
    if len(values)!=6 { rt_free(runtime(),values);return fail(env,900,1,0,0); }
    let mut seen:u64=0;
    let mut i:u64=0;
    while i<6 { values[i]=0;i=i+1; }
    let mut mask:u64=0;
    let mut ok:bool=true;
    let mut at_id:u64=kid(env,id);
    while at_id!=0 && ok {
        let entry:u64=at_id;
        if nkind(env,entry)==k_ct_limit() {
            let name_id:u64=kid(env,at_id);
            let value_id:u64=nth(env,at_id,1);
            let index:u64=limit_index(env,name_id);
            if index>5 { ok=false; }
            else {
                let bit:u64=1<<index;
                if (mask&bit)!=0 { ok=false; }
                else if live(env,value_id)==false { ok=false; }
                else {
                    let value_node:u64=value_id;
                    if nkind(env,value_node)!=k_literal() { ok=false; }
                    else if naux(env,value_node)>limit_maximum(index) { ok=false; }
                    else { mask=mask|bit;values[index]=naux(env,value_node); }
                }
            }
        }
        at_id=sib(env,at_id);
    }
    if ok && mask!=63 { ok=false; }
    if ok {
        limit_store(env,0,values[0]);limit_store(env,1,values[1]);
        limit_store(env,2,values[2]);limit_store(env,3,values[3]);
        limit_store(env,4,values[4]);limit_store(env,5,values[5]);
    }
    rt_free(runtime(),values);
    if ok==false { return fail(env,378,1,nline(env,id),ncolumn(env,id)); }
    return 0;
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------- macro lookup
// The one based declaration order of every macro in the module is its
// ordinal, so a hygiene mark depends only on the source text.
fn macro_ordinal(env:Env,id:u64)->u64 {
    let mut ordinal:u64=0;
    let mut scan:u64=1;
    while scan<=env.tree.node_count {
        let n:u64=scan;
        if nkind(env,n)==k_macro() {
            ordinal=ordinal+1;
            if scan==id { return ordinal; }
        }
        scan=scan+1;
    }
    return 0;
}

fn count_macros(env:Env)->u64 {
    let mut ordinal:u64=0;
    let mut scan:u64=1;
    while scan<=env.tree.node_count {
        if nkind(env,scan)==k_macro() { ordinal=ordinal+1; }
        scan=scan+1;
    }
    return ordinal;
}

fn find_macro(env:Env,name_id:u64)->u64 {
    if live(env,name_id)==false { return 0; }
    let want:u64=name_id;
    let mut scan:u64=1;
    while scan<=env.tree.node_count {
        let n:u64=scan;
        if nkind(env,n)==k_macro() {
            let declared:u64=kid(env,scan);
            if live(env,declared)==true {
                let dn:u64=declared;
                if same_text(env,nstart(env,dn),nend(env,dn),nstart(env,want),nend(env,want)) { return scan; }
            }
        }
        scan=scan+1;
    }
    return 0;
}

// A macro parameter kind: 1 ast.expr, 2 ast.type, 3 a comptime value.
fn parameter_kind(env:Env,param:u64)->u64 {
    let type_id:u64=nth(env,param,1);
    if live(env,type_id)==false { return 0; }
    let t:u64=type_id;
    if nkind(env,t)!=k_type() { return 0; }
    if naux(env,t)==5 {
        let inner:u64=kid(env,type_id);
        if word_id(env,inner,7) { return 1; }
        if word_id(env,inner,8) { return 2; }
        return 0;
    }
    return 3;
}

fn parameter_name(env:Env,param:u64)->u64 { return kid(env,param); }

fn parameter_type_node(env:Env,param:u64)->u64 { return nth(env,param,1); }

// The declared result kind, or 0 for a form outside this profile.
fn macro_result(env:Env,id:u64)->u64 {
    let mut at_id:u64=kid(env,id);
    while at_id!=0 {
        let n:u64=at_id;
        if nkind(env,n)==k_type() { return result_of(env,at_id); }
        if nkind(env,n)==k_meta_block() { return 0; }
        at_id=sib(env,at_id);
    }
    return 0;
}

fn result_of(env:Env,type_id:u64)->u64 {
    let t:u64=type_id;
    if naux(env,t)==5 {
        let inner:u64=kid(env,type_id);
        if word_id(env,inner,7) { return result_expr(); }
        if word_id(env,inner,9) { return result_stmt(); }
        if word_id(env,inner,10) { return result_decl(); }
        if word_id(env,inner,8) { return result_type(); }
        if word_id(env,inner,11) { return result_block(); }
        return 0;
    }
    return 0;
}

fn macro_parameters(env:Env,id:u64)->u64 {
    let mut count:u64=0;
    let mut at_id:u64=kid(env,id);
    while at_id!=0 {
        if nkind(env,at_id)==k_mparam() { count=count+1; }
        if nkind(env,at_id)==k_meta_block() { break; }
        at_id=sib(env,at_id);
    }
    return count;
}

fn macro_parameter_at(env:Env,id:u64,index:u64)->u64 {
    let mut seen:u64=0;
    let mut at_id:u64=kid(env,id);
    while at_id!=0 {
        if nkind(env,at_id)==k_meta_block() { return 0; }
        if nkind(env,at_id)==k_mparam() {
            if seen==index { return at_id; }
            seen=seen+1;
        }
        at_id=sib(env,at_id);
    }
    return 0;
}

fn macro_body(env:Env,id:u64)->u64 {
    let mut at_id:u64=kid(env,id);
    while at_id!=0 {
        if nkind(env,at_id)==k_meta_block() { return at_id; }
        at_id=sib(env,at_id);
    }
    return 0;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------- hygiene marks
// Every mark is built by textual composition in the name table, so the result
// is a pure function of the source, the ordinal and the expansion serial.
// The fixed text fragments of a hygiene mark, selected by number so that no
// call site has to pass a literal where a buffer is expected.
fn put_mark(env:Env,which:u64,line:u64,column:u64)->u64 {
    if which==1 { return put_words(env,6,0x0000246d746e5f5f,0x0000000000000000,0x0000000000000000,line,column); }
    if which==2 { return put_words(env,6,0x00002464746e5f5f,0x0000000000000000,0x0000000000000000,line,column); }
    if which==3 { return put_words(env,6,0x00002470746e5f5f,0x0000000000000000,0x0000000000000000,line,column); }
    if which==4 { return put_words(env,2,0x0000000000006524,0x0000000000000000,0x0000000000000000,line,column); }
    if which==5 { return put_words(env,2,0x0000000000007224,0x0000000000000000,0x0000000000000000,line,column); }
    if which==6 { return put_words(env,2,0x0000000000007424,0x0000000000000000,0x0000000000000000,line,column); }
    if which==7 { return put_words(env,2,0x0000000000007024,0x0000000000000000,0x0000000000000000,line,column); }
    if which==8 { return put_words(env,1,0x0000000000000024,0x0000000000000000,0x0000000000000000,line,column); }
    return fail(env,800,2,line,column);
}

// Every mark is built by textual composition in the name table, so the result
// is a pure function of the source, the ordinal and the expansion serial.
fn mark_local(env:Env,ordinal:u64,serial:u64,name:u64,
              inv:u64)->u64 {
    let start:u64=env.name_bytes;
    if put_mark(env,1,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
    if put_number(env,ordinal,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
    if put_mark(env,4,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
    if put_number(env,serial,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
    if put_mark(env,8,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
    if put_text(env,env.source,nstart(env,name),nend(env,name),nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
    return start;
}

fn mark_decl(env:Env,ordinal:u64,serial:u64,repeat:u64,item:u64,listed:bool,
             name:u64,inv:u64)->u64 {
    let start:u64=env.name_bytes;
    if put_mark(env,2,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
    if put_number(env,ordinal,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
    if put_mark(env,4,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
    if put_number(env,serial,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
    if listed {
        if put_mark(env,5,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
        if put_number(env,repeat,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
        if put_mark(env,6,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
        if put_number(env,item,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
    }
    if put_mark(env,8,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
    if put_text(env,env.source,nstart(env,name),nend(env,name),nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
    return start;
}

fn mark_param(env:Env,ordinal:u64,serial:u64,index:u64,name:u64,
              inv:u64)->u64 {
    let start:u64=env.name_bytes;
    if put_mark(env,3,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
    if put_number(env,ordinal,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
    if put_mark(env,4,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
    if put_number(env,serial,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
    if put_mark(env,7,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
    if put_number(env,index,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
    if put_mark(env,8,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
    if put_text(env,env.source,nstart(env,name),nend(env,name),nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
    return start;
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------ value checks
// A comptime value parameter accepts a plain integer literal, or the name
// true or false for a bool, and the value must fit the declared type. The
// wider evaluator is not used for arguments, so an argument can never carry a
// name, a call or a comparison into the template.
fn value_fits(env:Env,type_id:u64,negative:bool,magnitude:u64)->bool {
    let name_id:u64=kid(env,type_id);
    if word_id(env,name_id,12) {
        if negative { return false; }
        if magnitude>1 { return false; }
        return true;
    }
    let mut bits:u64=0;
    let mut signed:bool=false;
    if word_id(env,name_id,13) { bits=8;signed=false; }
    else if word_id(env,name_id,14) { bits=16;signed=false; }
    else if word_id(env,name_id,15) { bits=32;signed=false; }
    else if word_id(env,name_id,16) { bits=64;signed=false; }
    else if word_id(env,name_id,17) { bits=8;signed=true; }
    else if word_id(env,name_id,18) { bits=16;signed=true; }
    else if word_id(env,name_id,19) { bits=32;signed=true; }
    else if word_id(env,name_id,20) { bits=64;signed=true; }
    else { return false; }
    if signed {
        let half:u64=1<<(bits-1);
        if negative {
            if magnitude>half { return false; }
            return true;
        }
        if magnitude>=half { return false; }
        return true;
    }
    if negative { return false; }
    if bits==64 { return true; }
    let top:u64=1<<bits;
    if magnitude>=top { return false; }
    return true;
}

// Fold an expression that the module itself produced. The only evaluator is
// constant_eval, so no second arithmetic exists here.
fn fold(env:Env,id:u64)->constant_eval.Value {
    return constant_eval.evaluate(produced(env),id);
}

// Materialise a folded constant as produced literal nodes. A negative value
// becomes the unary minus of its magnitude, which is the only shape
// constant_eval reads back as a negative value.
//
// source_id is the CALLER node the constant was folded from: the argument of a
// value parameter, or the expression a comptime let bound, or the count a
// repeat runs to. It is what the literal's token is taken from, because a
// token taken from the template would name the $v of the splice position --
// a NAME token -- and ntir.type_literal would refuse the whole image in 817
// on a module the pipeline accepts. See the block above for the whole argument.
fn emit_constant(env:Env,negative:bool,magnitude:u64,origin:u64,
                 inv:u64,source_id:u64)->u64 {
    if charge_constant(env,8,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
    let bias:u64=folded_token(env,source_id);
    if negative {
        let outer:u64=emit(env,k_expr(),45,origin,inv,nstart(env,inv),nend(env,inv));
        if outer==0 { return 0; }
        let inner:u64=emit(env,k_literal(),magnitude,origin,inv,nstart(env,inv),nend(env,inv));
        if inner==0 { return 0; }
        // Only the literal is restamped. The unary minus is a kind 35 whose
        // token no pass reads: ntir.nova:652-689 has no arm for an expression,
        // so it types nothing and reads no token.
        stamp_literal_token(env,inner,bias);
        prod_attach(env,outer,inner);
        return outer;
    }
    let made:u64=emit(env,k_literal(),magnitude,origin,inv,nstart(env,inv),nend(env,inv));
    if made==0 { return 0; }
    stamp_literal_token(env,made,bias);
    return made;
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------- the context
// Everything one expansion needs to instantiate a template.
struct Ctx {
    macro:u64,arg_base:u64,ordinal:u64,serial:u64,
    repeat:u64,item:u64,listed:bool,result:u64,frame:u64
}

fn empty_ctx()->Ctx {
    return Ctx{macro:0,arg_base:0,ordinal:0,serial:0,repeat:0,item:0,
        listed:false,result:0,frame:0};
}

// The expander is mutually recursive: a template can hold a nested expand,
// whose body can hold another one. Every such step is bounded by the call
// depth, expansion depth and fuel budgets, never by the language.

// Does the caller declare this name outside any macro body? A template is not
// a caller declaration: the name it introduces lives inside a macro and is
// renamed, so scanning it would collide with the template itself. The walk
// descends from the module root and skips a macro body whole.
//
// THE TRUE AT THE CAP IS A REFUSAL, AND IT IS WHY IT IS TRUE, NOT A LIE ABOUT
// THE SCAN. This predicate is "the caller declares this name", so a cap
// crossing cannot answer false -- answering false would say the scan looked
// and found nothing, which is the one answer this site must never give, since
// the scan did not look. True is the conservative refusal: refuse the
// expansion as though a collision existed, so no unreached name is ever
// renamed into an existing one. The three callers all read true as
// fail(381,1), so the cap produces a red, not a green.
//
// WHAT WAS WRONG, AND IT IS NOT THE POLARITY. The refusal was a truthful
// refusal under a code that says something else: 381/1 means "the caller
// declares this name", and a cap crossing claims exactly that with no
// declaration anywhere in sight. Before this change the two cases were
// byte-identical on the wire -- same code, same reason, and out[10]/out[11]
// both 0, which the export's own comment reserves for a refusal that is NOT a
// bound. So a reader could not tell a real collision from a scan cut short at
// 64, and "the two are told apart" was false at this site: the channel that
// tells them apart was exactly the one this site left empty.
//
// So the two words are written here under note_bound's guard, which is
// fail()'s guard, and the caller raises 381/1 as it always did. The refusal a
// caller reads keeps its code and gains the quantity, which is the
// reserve_nodes/reserve_names precedent and not a new mechanism. Nothing runs
// between the write and the caller's fail: caller_declares charges its fuel
// BEFORE this call, and on the way out it only returns.
fn declares_below(env:Env,id:u64,start:u64,end:u64,depth:u64)->bool {
    if depth>walk_depth_cap() {
        note_bound(env,depth,walk_depth_cap());
        return true;
    }
    let mut at_id:u64=id;
    while at_id!=0 {
        if live(env,at_id)==false { return true; }
        let n:u64=at_id;
        if nkind(env,n)==k_macro() || nkind(env,n)==k_comptime() { at_id=sib(env,at_id);continue; }
        if nkind(env,n)==k_const() || nkind(env,n)==k_data() || nkind(env,n)==k_fn() ||
           nkind(env,n)==k_param() {
            let declared:u64=kid(env,at_id);
            if live(env,declared)==true {
                let dn:u64=declared;
                if same_text(env,nstart(env,dn),nend(env,dn),start,end) { return true; }
            }
        }
        if declares_below(env,kid(env,at_id),start,end,depth+1) { return true; }
        at_id=sib(env,at_id);
    }
    return false;
}

fn caller_declares(env:Env,start:u64,end:u64)->bool {
    if charge_fuel(env,0,0)!=0 { return true; }
    return declares_below(env,kid(env,1),start,end,0);
}

// The parameter index a template name refers to, or 99 when it is not a macro
// parameter. Parameters are matched on their template spelling, which is
// unique because a duplicate parameter name is refused.
fn parameter_index(env:Env,ctx:Ctx,name_id:u64)->u64 {
    if live(env,name_id)==false { return 99; }
    let want:u64=name_id;
    let count:u64=macro_parameters(env,ctx.macro);
    let mut index:u64=0;
    while index<count {
        let param:u64=macro_parameter_at(env,ctx.macro,index);
        let declared:u64=parameter_name(env,param);
        if live(env,declared)==true {
            let dn:u64=declared;
            if same_text(env,nstart(env,dn),nend(env,dn),nstart(env,want),nend(env,want)) { return index; }
        }
        index=index+1;
    }
    return 99;
}

// ---- WHICH FORBIDDEN CHANNEL A COMPTIME EXPRESSION REACHED FOR ---------
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS, and it is a defect that was measured rather than imagined.
// Before it, EVERY attempt to name an I/O channel inside a comptime
// expression was refused 374 reason 1, "capture of a caller symbol" -- the
// hygiene rule, which fires because the name is unbound and not because it is
// a channel. rt_open_read, rt_write, rt_diag and a name that is not a
// primitive at all all answered the same code and the same reason, so the
// refusal carried no information about the channel: a reader could not tell
// which one fired, and a gate that asserted "the file-read channel is refused
// at comptime" was in fact asserting that unbound names are refused.
//
// WHAT THE CLASS IS, and why it is not the presence of a word. These six
// spellings are not a vocabulary this module invented: they are the Nova
// language's own primitive table, the runtime_names[] array the front end
// recognises as syntax (nova.c, the seven-name runtime table) and the one the
// host runtime implements with fopen/fread/fwrite (runtime_v2). A spelling is
// a channel because the ENVIRONMENT publishes it as one, and the gate that
// ships with this lot re-reads that table out of the environment's own source
// and fails if the two disagree. rt_free, alloc, rt_copy, slice, runtime and
// len are in the same table and are NOT listed here: they are the permitted
// half, and the classifier refusing them would be a blanket refusal wearing a
// channel's name.
//
// The four reasons are DISTINCT NUMBERS, not one answer under four labels, and
// they are published as the reason of 379 beside the evaluator statuses 1-8.
fn channel_file_read()->u64 { return 20; }
fn channel_file_write()->u64 { return 21; }
fn channel_file_close()->u64 { return 22; }
fn channel_diagnostic()->u64 { return 23; }

// The class of one name, or 0 when the name is not a channel primitive. The
// packing is text_is's own, stated once in that function: byte i of the text
// is bit (i%8)*8 of word i/8.
fn channel_id(env:Env,id:u64)->u64 {
    // rt_open_read, rt_read -- open an existing file for reading, read bytes.
    if named(env,id,12,0x5f6e65706f5f7472,0x0000000064616572,0x0000000000000000)==true { return channel_file_read(); }
    if named(env,id,7,0x00646165725f7472,0x0000000000000000,0x0000000000000000)==true { return channel_file_read(); }
    // rt_open_new, rt_write -- create a file, write bytes into it.
    if named(env,id,11,0x5f6e65706f5f7472,0x000000000077656e,0x0000000000000000)==true { return channel_file_write(); }
    if named(env,id,8,0x65746972775f7472,0x0000000000000000,0x0000000000000000)==true { return channel_file_write(); }
    // rt_close -- the lifecycle half of the file channel, refused on its own
    // number so a reader is never left guessing which of the three fired.
    if named(env,id,8,0x65736f6c635f7472,0x0000000000000000,0x0000000000000000)==true { return channel_file_close(); }
    // rt_diag -- diagnostic output on the host's stderr.
    if named(env,id,7,0x00676169645f7472,0x0000000000000000,0x0000000000000000)==true { return channel_diagnostic(); }
    return 0;
}

// THE FIRST CHANNEL PRIMITIVE IN THE CALLER SUBTREE, or 0.
//
// This is the second of the two doors the attempt can come through, and it is
// the one the first does not see. channel_id classifies a name the TEMPLATE
// spells at a binding position, which resolve_name below reaches while
// cloning. An attempt can also arrive SPELCED: the caller writes the channel
// call as an ast.expr macro argument, so the TEMPLATE holds only the $name and
// the spelling lives in the caller's own node. A splice is followed here
// exactly as clone_splice follows it -- same parameter_index, same slot, same
// arg_node tag -- because a walk that stopped at the $name would answer 0 on
// the one route where the author never spelled the channel in the template at
// all, and that refusal would fall back to the evaluator's own reason, which
// names a shape and not a channel.
//
// The walk is over the CALLER tree, never the produced one. A produced node
// carries the invocation's span, not the span of the caller's own expression,
// so a produced tree cannot name the spelling -- that is a measured fact
// about emit, not a choice made here.
fn fold_channel(env:Env,ctx:Ctx,id:u64,depth:u64)->u64 {
    if live(env,id)==false || depth>walk_depth_cap() { return 0; }
    let n:u64=id;
    if nkind(env,n)==k_name() { return channel_id(env,n); }
    if nkind(env,n)==k_expr() && naux(env,n)==36 {
        let name_id:u64=kid(env,n);
        if live(env,name_id)==false { return 0; }
        let index:u64=parameter_index(env,ctx,name_id);
        if index>=macro_parameters(env,ctx.macro) { return 0; }
        let param:u64=macro_parameter_at(env,ctx.macro,index);
        if parameter_kind(env,param)!=1 { return 0; }
        let slot:u64=ctx.arg_base+index*arg_stride();
        if slot+arg_stride()>env.arg_top { return 0; }
        if arg_tag(env,slot)!=arg_node() { return 0; }
        return fold_channel(env,ctx,arg_first(env,slot),depth+1);
    }
    let count:u64=child_count(env,id);
    let mut at:u64=0;
    while at<count {
        let found:u64=fold_channel(env,ctx,nth(env,id,at),depth+1);
        if found!=0 { return found; }
        at=at+1;
    }
    return 0;
}

// Resolve a binding position name. Returns the produced node, or 0 after a
// refusal. There is no fourth outcome: a name that is not bound inside the
// template is a capture attempt and is refused.
//
// THE ONE PLACE A CAPTURE BECOMES A CHANNEL REFUSAL. env.folding is set only
// around the clone that produces an expression the expander is about to FOLD,
// so a name elsewhere in a template keeps 374 exactly as before -- a template
// that merely spells rt_write is a hygiene capture and this module must not
// call that an I/O attempt. Inside a fold it is a different act: the expander
// is about to ACQUIRE a value from the environment, and the name it reached
// for says which environment capability it reached for.
fn resolve_name(env:Env,ctx:Ctx,name_id:u64,inv:u64,depth:u64,
                as_type:bool)->u64 {
    let name:u64=name_id;
    if binding_exists(env,name_id)==true {
        let slot:u64=bind_offset(env,name_id,true,true,true);
        if slot==0 { return fail(env,373,1,nline(env,name),ncolumn(env,name)); }
        let value:u64=slot-1;
        if bind_kind(env,value)==bind_value() {
            return emit_constant(env,bind_negative(env,value),
                bind_magnitude(env,value),name,inv,bind_origin(env,value));
        }
        return emit_name(env,name,inv,bind_start(env,value),
            bind_length(env,value));
    }
    if env.folding==true {
        let channel:u64=channel_id(env,name);
        if channel!=0 { return fail(env,379,channel,nline(env,name),ncolumn(env,name)); }
    }
    return fail(env,374,1,nline(env,name),ncolumn(env,name));
}

// A qualified name: the leading segments are spelling, and only the last one
// is a binding position when the qualified name is a symbol reference.
fn clone_qualified(env:Env,ctx:Ctx,id:u64,inv:u64,depth:u64,
                   last_is_bind:u64)->u64 {
    let n:u64=id;
    if charge_fuel(env,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
    let out:u64=emit(env,k_qualified(),naux(env,n),n,inv,nstart(env,inv),nend(env,inv));
    if out==0 { return 0; }
    let count:u64=child_count(env,id);
    let mut index:u64=0;
    while index<count {
        let child:u64=nth(env,id,index);
        let mut pos:u64=pos_spelled();
        if last_is_bind!=0 && (index+1)==count { pos=pos_bind(); }
        let piece:u64=clone(env,ctx,child,pos,inv,depth+1);
        if piece==0 { return 0; }
        prod_attach(env,out,piece);
        index=index+1;
    }
    return out;
}

fn clone_children(env:Env,ctx:Ctx,out:u64,id:u64,pos:u64,inv:u64,
                  depth:u64)->u64 {
    let count:u64=child_count(env,id);
    let mut index:u64=0;
    while index<count {
        let child:u64=nth(env,id,index);
        let piece:u64=clone(env,ctx,child,pos,inv,depth+1);
        if piece==0 { return 0; }
        prod_attach(env,out,piece);
        index=index+1;
    }
    return out;
}

// A let statement introduces a symbol, so it is renamed and the later
// references to it are rewritten. The mark is __ntm$ as in frontend.c:2384.
fn clone_let(env:Env,ctx:Ctx,id:u64,inv:u64,depth:u64)->u64 {
    let n:u64=id;
    let name_id:u64=kid(env,id);
    let type_id:u64=nth(env,id,1);
    let init_id:u64=nth(env,id,2);
    if live(env,name_id)==false || live(env,type_id)==false ||
       live(env,init_id)==false {
        return fail(env,800,1,nline(env,n),ncolumn(env,n));
    }
    let name:u64=name_id;
    if binding_exists(env,name_id)==true {
        return fail(env,376,1,nline(env,name),ncolumn(env,name));
    }
    if parameter_index(env,ctx,name_id)<macro_parameters(env,ctx.macro) {
        return fail(env,376,2,nline(env,name),ncolumn(env,name));
    }
    if reserved_word(env,name_id) { return fail(env,376,3,nline(env,name),ncolumn(env,name)); }
    if caller_declares(env,nstart(env,name),nend(env,name)) {
        return fail(env,381,1,nline(env,name),ncolumn(env,name));
    }
    let start:u64=mark_local(env,ctx.ordinal,ctx.serial,name,inv);
    if start==0 && env.code!=0 { return 0; }
    let length:u64=env.name_bytes-start;
    let out:u64=emit(env,k_let(),naux(env,n),n,inv,nstart(env,inv),nend(env,inv));
    if out==0 { return 0; }
    if push_binding(env,bind_local(),false,0,start,length,name_id,0)!=0 {
        return 0;
    }
    let fresh:u64=emit_name(env,name,inv,start,length);
    if fresh==0 { return 0; }
    prod_attach(env,out,fresh);
    let type_copy:u64=clone(env,ctx,type_id,pos_spelled(),inv,depth+1);
    if type_copy==0 { return 0; }
    prod_attach(env,out,type_copy);
    let init_copy:u64=clone(env,ctx,init_id,pos_bind(),inv,depth+1);
    if init_copy==0 { return 0; }
    prod_attach(env,out,init_copy);
    return out;
}

// A memory operand holds register and space spellings, displacements and one
// type. Only the type is a typed position and a displacement is a constant.
fn clone_memory(env:Env,ctx:Ctx,id:u64,inv:u64,depth:u64)->u64 {
    let n:u64=id;
    if charge_fuel(env,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
    let out:u64=emit(env,k_memory(),naux(env,n),n,inv,nstart(env,inv),nend(env,inv));
    if out==0 { return 0; }
    let count:u64=child_count(env,id);
    let mut index:u64=0;
    while index<count {
        let child:u64=nth(env,id,index);
        let cn:u64=child;
        let mut pos:u64=pos_spelled();
        if nkind(env,cn)==k_expr() { pos=pos_bind(); }
        let piece:u64=clone(env,ctx,child,pos,inv,depth+1);
        if piece==0 { return 0; }
        prod_attach(env,out,piece);
        index=index+1;
    }
    return out;
}

// A type. A $T splice resolves to the caller argument node, never to a re-parse
// of its spelling.
fn clone_type(env:Env,ctx:Ctx,id:u64,inv:u64,depth:u64)->u64 {
    let n:u64=id;
    if naux(env,n)==5 { return fail(env,375,1,nline(env,n),ncolumn(env,n)); }
    if naux(env,n)==3 { return fail(env,386,1,nline(env,n),ncolumn(env,n)); }
    if naux(env,n)==4 {
        let name_id:u64=kid(env,id);
        let index:u64=parameter_index(env,ctx,name_id);
        if index<macro_parameters(env,ctx.macro) {
            let slot:u64=ctx.arg_base+index*arg_stride();
            if slot+arg_stride()<=env.arg_top {
                if arg_tag(env,slot)==3 { return clone_argument_context(env,ctx,slot,inv,depth); }
                return clone(env,ctx,arg_first(env,slot),pos_caller(),inv,depth+1);
            }
        }
        return fail(env,372,1,nline(env,name_id),ncolumn(env,name_id));
    }
    if charge_fuel(env,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
    let out:u64=emit(env,k_type(),naux(env,n),n,inv,nstart(env,inv),nend(env,inv));
    if out==0 { return 0; }
    let count:u64=child_count(env,id);
    let mut index:u64=0;
    while index<count {
        let child:u64=nth(env,id,index);
        let piece:u64=clone(env,ctx,child,pos_spelled(),inv,depth+1);
        if piece==0 { return 0; }
        prod_attach(env,out,piece);
        index=index+1;
    }
    return out;
}

// A $name splice. A macro parameter splices the caller typed AST node for the
// matching argument, a comptime value materialises its folded constant and a
// hygienic name rewrites to its fresh text. Anything else is a capture.
// Frames are bounded by the existing expansion-depth limit. Each active frame
// records its macro, argument base and lexical binding interval. Argument
// records remain four words: tag 3 refers to an ancestor frame, not a copied AST.
fn ensure_frames(env:Env)->u64 {
    let old:buf<u64>=env.frames;
    if len(old)!=0 { return 0; }
    let count:u64=max_expansion_depth()*4;
    let words:buf<u64>=alloc(runtime(),count);
    if len(words)!=count { rt_free(runtime(),words);return fail(env,900,1,0,0); }
    env.frames=words;rt_free(runtime(),old);return 0;
}
fn clone_argument_context(env:Env,ctx:Ctx,slot:u64,inv:u64,depth:u64)->u64 {
    let frame:u64=arg_second(env,slot);
    if frame==0||frame>=env.depth { fail(env,800,1,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv));return 0; }
    let frames:buf<u64>=env.frames;
    let at:u64=(frame-1)*4;
    if at+4>len(frames) { fail(env,800,1,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv));return 0; }
    let old_macro:u64=ctx.macro;let old_base:u64=ctx.arg_base;let old_frame:u64=ctx.frame;
    let old_scope:u64=env.scope_base;let old_top:u64=env.bind_top;
    ctx.macro=frames[at];ctx.arg_base=frames[at+1];ctx.frame=frame;
    env.scope_base=frames[at+2];env.bind_top=frames[at+3];
    let made:u64=clone(env,ctx,arg_first(env,slot),pos_bind(),inv,depth+1);
    ctx.macro=old_macro;ctx.arg_base=old_base;ctx.frame=old_frame;
    env.scope_base=old_scope;env.bind_top=old_top;
    return made;
}

fn clone_splice(env:Env,ctx:Ctx,id:u64,inv:u64,depth:u64,
                as_type:bool)->u64 {
    let n:u64=id;
    let name_id:u64=kid(env,id);
    if live(env,name_id)==false { return fail(env,800,1,nline(env,n),ncolumn(env,n)); }
    let index:u64=parameter_index(env,ctx,name_id);
    if index<macro_parameters(env,ctx.macro) {
        let param:u64=macro_parameter_at(env,ctx.macro,index);
        let kind:u64=parameter_kind(env,param);
        let slot:u64=ctx.arg_base+index*arg_stride();
        if slot+arg_stride()>env.arg_top {
            return fail(env,372,1,nline(env,name_id),ncolumn(env,name_id));
        }
        // A comptime value parameter carries its folded sign and magnitude, so
        // a splice of it materialises that constant, never a node.
        if kind==3 {
            if as_type { return fail(env,372,1,nline(env,name_id),ncolumn(env,name_id)); }
            if arg_tag(env,slot)!=arg_value() {
                return fail(env,372,1,nline(env,name_id),ncolumn(env,name_id));
            }
            return emit_constant(env,arg_first(env,slot)==1,arg_second(env,slot),
                name_id,inv,arg_origin(env,slot));
        }
        if (as_type&&kind==2) || ((as_type==false)&&kind==1) {
            // An argument the ENCLOSING template bound carries its mark, and
            // the mark is what the inner expansion must publish: the same
            // fresh text the outer published, so the reference crosses the
            // boundary pointing at the binding the author introduced. The
            // caller-node arm below is unchanged and still correct -- a
            // caller owns its names, so those keep their spelling.
            if arg_tag(env,slot)==3 { return clone_argument_context(env,ctx,slot,inv,depth); }
            if arg_tag(env,slot)==arg_mark() {
                return emit_name(env,arg_origin(env,slot),inv,
                                 arg_first(env,slot),arg_second(env,slot));
            }
            return clone(env,ctx,arg_first(env,slot),pos_caller(),inv,depth+1);
        }
        return fail(env,372,1,nline(env,name_id),ncolumn(env,name_id));
    }
    return resolve_name(env,ctx,name_id,inv,depth,as_type);
}

// A symbol reference or a call target: the last segment of a qualified name
// is the binding, the leading segments are spelling.
fn clone_symbol(env:Env,ctx:Ctx,id:u64,inv:u64,depth:u64)->u64 {
    if live(env,id)==false { return fail(env,800,1,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv)); }
    if nkind(env,id)==k_qualified() {
        return clone_qualified(env,ctx,id,inv,depth,1);
    }
    return clone(env,ctx,id,pos_bind(),inv,depth+1);
}

fn clone(env:Env,ctx:Ctx,id:u64,pos:u64,inv:u64,depth:u64)->u64 {
    if depth>walk_depth_cap() { return refuse_bound(env,375,9,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv),depth,walk_depth_cap()); }
    if live(env,id)==false { return fail(env,800,1,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv)); }
    if env.code!=0 { return 0; }
    let n:u64=id;
    if nkind(env,n)==k_literal() {
        if charge_constant(env,nend(env,n)-nstart(env,n),nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
        return emit(env,k_literal(),naux(env,n),n,inv,nstart(env,inv),nend(env,inv));
    }
    if nkind(env,n)==k_name() {
        if pos!=pos_bind() { return emit_name_copy(env,n,inv); }
        return resolve_name(env,ctx,id,inv,depth,false);
    }
    if nkind(env,n)==k_qualified() { return clone_qualified(env,ctx,id,inv,depth,0); }
    // The effects, clobbers and abi of a produced declaration are carried over
    // verbatim: their entries are target spellings, not bindings, and they are
    // re-checked by the normal effects, clobbers and abi passes afterwards.
    if nkind(env,n)==k_effects() || nkind(env,n)==k_clobbers() {
        if charge_fuel(env,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
        let out:u64=emit(env,nkind(env,n),naux(env,n),n,inv,nstart(env,inv),nend(env,inv));
        if out==0 { return 0; }
        return clone_children(env,ctx,out,id,pos_spelled(),inv,depth);
    }
    if nkind(env,n)==k_dashed() || nkind(env,n)==k_section_name() || is_abi(env,id)==true {
        if charge_fuel(env,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
        let out:u64=emit(env,nkind(env,n),naux(env,n),n,inv,nstart(env,inv),nend(env,inv));
        if out==0 { return 0; }
        return clone_children(env,ctx,out,id,pos_spelled(),inv,depth);
    }
    if nkind(env,n)==k_type() { return clone_type(env,ctx,id,inv,depth); }
    if nkind(env,n)==k_expr() {
        if naux(env,n)==36 { return clone_splice(env,ctx,id,inv,depth,false); }
        if charge_fuel(env,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
        let out:u64=emit(env,k_expr(),naux(env,n),n,inv,nstart(env,inv),nend(env,inv));
        if out==0 { return 0; }
        let count:u64=child_count(env,id);
        let mut index:u64=0;
        while index<count {
            let child:u64=nth(env,id,index);
            if (naux(env,n)==0 || naux(env,n)==1) && index==0 && pos!=pos_caller() {
                let piece:u64=clone_symbol(env,ctx,child,inv,depth+1);
                if piece==0 { return 0; }
                prod_attach(env,out,piece);
                index=index+1;
                continue;
            }
            let piece:u64=clone(env,ctx,child,pos,inv,depth+1);
            if piece==0 { return 0; }
            prod_attach(env,out,piece);
            index=index+1;
        }
        return out;
    }
    if nkind(env,n)>=k_block() && nkind(env,n)<=k_memory() {
        return clone_stmt(env,ctx,id,pos,inv,depth);
    }
    return fail(env,375,1,nline(env,n),ncolumn(env,n));
}

fn clone_stmt(env:Env,ctx:Ctx,id:u64,pos:u64,inv:u64,depth:u64)->u64 {
    let n:u64=id;
    if nkind(env,n)==k_block() {
        if charge_fuel(env,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
        let out:u64=emit(env,k_block(),naux(env,n),n,inv,nstart(env,inv),nend(env,inv));
        if out==0 { return 0; }
        let save:u64=env.bind_top;
        let count:u64=child_count(env,id);
        let mut index:u64=0;
        while index<count {
            let child:u64=nth(env,id,index);
            let piece:u64=clone(env,ctx,child,pos_bind(),inv,depth+1);
            if piece==0 { env.bind_top=save;return 0; }
            prod_attach(env,out,piece);
            index=index+1;
        }
        env.bind_top=save;return out;
    }
    if nkind(env,n)==k_let() { return clone_let(env,ctx,id,inv,depth); }
    if nkind(env,n)==k_call() { return clone_call(env,ctx,id,pos,inv,depth); }
    if nkind(env,n)==k_return() {
        if charge_fuel(env,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
        let out:u64=emit(env,k_return(),naux(env,n),n,inv,nstart(env,inv),nend(env,inv));
        if out==0 { return 0; }
        return clone_children(env,ctx,out,id,pos_bind(),inv,depth);
    }
    if nkind(env,n)==k_if() {
        if charge_fuel(env,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
        let out:u64=emit(env,k_if(),naux(env,n),n,inv,nstart(env,inv),nend(env,inv));
        if out==0 { return 0; }
        return clone_children(env,ctx,out,id,pos_bind(),inv,depth);
    }
    if nkind(env,n)==k_expand() { return expand_node(env,ctx.frame,id,depth+1); }
    if nkind(env,n)==k_insn() {
        if naux(env,n)==1 { return fail(env,375,2,nline(env,n),ncolumn(env,n)); }
        if charge_fuel(env,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
        let out:u64=emit(env,k_insn(),0,n,inv,nstart(env,inv),nend(env,inv));
        if out==0 { return 0; }
        let count:u64=child_count(env,id);
        let mut index:u64=0;
        while index<count {
            let child:u64=nth(env,id,index);
            let mut child_pos:u64=pos_bind();
            if index==0 { child_pos=pos_spelled(); }
            let piece:u64=clone(env,ctx,child,child_pos,inv,depth+1);
            if piece==0 { return 0; }
            prod_attach(env,out,piece);
            index=index+1;
        }
        return out;
    }
    if nkind(env,n)==k_call() { return clone_call(env,ctx,id,pos,inv,depth); }
    if nkind(env,n)==k_memory() { return clone_memory(env,ctx,id,inv,depth); }
    return fail(env,375,1,nline(env,n),ncolumn(env,n));
}

// A call target is a symbol reference, so it is a binding position and the
// callee is resolved against the macro bindings. Only a call that was itself
// spliced in from a caller argument keeps the caller spelling.
fn clone_call(env:Env,ctx:Ctx,id:u64,pos:u64,inv:u64,depth:u64)->u64 {
    let n:u64=id;
    if charge_fuel(env,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
    let out:u64=emit(env,k_call(),naux(env,n),n,inv,nstart(env,inv),nend(env,inv));
    if out==0 { return 0; }
    let count:u64=child_count(env,id);
    let mut index:u64=0;
    while index<count {
        let child:u64=nth(env,id,index);
        if index==0 && pos==pos_caller() {
            let piece:u64=clone(env,ctx,child,pos,inv,depth+1);
            if piece==0 { return 0; }
            prod_attach(env,out,piece);
            index=index+1;
            continue;
        }
        if index==0 {
            let symbol:u64=clone_symbol(env,ctx,child,inv,depth+1);
            if symbol==0 { return 0; }
            prod_attach(env,out,symbol);
            index=index+1;
            continue;
        }
        let piece:u64=clone(env,ctx,child,pos,inv,depth+1);
        if piece==0 { return 0; }
        prod_attach(env,out,piece);
        index=index+1;
    }
    return out;
}

// A generated function declaration. Parameters are renamed with __ntp$ and
// bound, so every body reference is rewritten. A requires or ensures clause is
// refused: a macro may not fabricate a proof.
fn produce_function(env:Env,ctx:Ctx,id:u64,inv:u64,depth:u64)->u64 {
    let n:u64=id;
    let name_id:u64=kid(env,id);
    if live(env,name_id)==false { return fail(env,800,1,nline(env,n),ncolumn(env,n)); }
    let name:u64=name_id;
    if binding_exists(env,name_id)==true {
        return fail(env,376,1,nline(env,name),ncolumn(env,name));
    }
    if reserved_word(env,name_id) { return fail(env,376,3,nline(env,name),ncolumn(env,name)); }
    if caller_declares(env,nstart(env,name),nend(env,name)) {
        return fail(env,381,1,nline(env,name),ncolumn(env,name));
    }
    let start:u64=mark_decl(env,ctx.ordinal,ctx.serial,ctx.repeat,ctx.item,
        ctx.listed,name,inv);
    if start==0 && env.code!=0 { return 0; }
    if charge_fuel(env,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
    let out:u64=emit(env,k_fn(),0,n,inv,nstart(env,inv),nend(env,inv));
    if out==0 { return 0; }
    let length:u64=env.name_bytes-start;
    if push_binding(env,bind_decl(),false,0,start,length,name_id,0)!=0 {
        return 0;
    }
    let fresh:u64=emit_name(env,name,inv,start,length);
    if fresh==0 { return 0; }
    prod_attach(env,out,fresh);
    let count:u64=child_count(env,id);
    let mut index:u64=1;
    let mut param_index:u64=0;
    while index<count {
        let child:u64=nth(env,id,index);
        let cn:u64=child;
        if nkind(env,cn)==k_param() {
            let pname_id:u64=kid(env,child);
            if live(env,pname_id)==false { return fail(env,800,1,nline(env,cn),ncolumn(env,cn)); }
            let pname:u64=pname_id;
            if binding_exists(env,pname_id)==true {
                return fail(env,376,1,nline(env,pname),ncolumn(env,pname));
            }
            if reserved_word(env,pname_id) {
                return fail(env,376,3,nline(env,pname),ncolumn(env,pname));
            }
            let pstart:u64=mark_param(env,ctx.ordinal,ctx.serial,param_index,pname,inv);
            if pstart==0 && env.code!=0 { return 0; }
            let plength:u64=env.name_bytes-pstart;
            if push_binding(env,bind_local(),false,0,pstart,plength,pname_id,0)!=0 {
                return 0;
            }
            if charge_fuel(env,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
            let pout:u64=emit(env,k_param(),naux(env,cn),cn,inv,nstart(env,inv),nend(env,inv));
            if pout==0 { return 0; }
            let pcopy:u64=emit_name(env,pname,inv,pstart,plength);
            if pcopy==0 { return 0; }
            prod_attach(env,pout,pcopy);
            let pcount:u64=child_count(env,child);
            let mut p:u64=1;
            while p<pcount {
                let pchild:u64=nth(env,child,p);
                let ppiece:u64=clone(env,ctx,pchild,pos_spelled(),inv,depth+1);
                if ppiece==0 { return 0; }
                prod_attach(env,pout,ppiece);
                p=p+1;
            }
            prod_attach(env,out,pout);
            param_index=param_index+1;
        } else if nkind(env,cn)==k_requires() || nkind(env,cn)==k_ensures() {
            return fail(env,375,3,nline(env,cn),ncolumn(env,cn));
        } else {
            // A generated function's body is ONE block child, and the
            // reference bounds what is inside it: frontend.c:3654 refuses the
            // 513th statement of a generated function body with NTF_E_LIMIT
            // "generated function statement limit exceeded", at that
            // statement's own token. The cap is applied HERE, on that block,
            // and not in clone_stmt, because a k_block nested anywhere else
            // in a template is a form the reference cannot express and a cap
            // there would be one this module invented.
            if nkind(env,cn)==k_block() {
                template_items(env,cn,0);
                if env.code!=0 { return 0; }
            }
            let piece:u64=clone(env,ctx,child,pos_spelled(),inv,depth+1);
            if piece==0 { return 0; }
            prod_attach(env,out,piece);
        }
        index=index+1;
    }
    return out;
}

// A generated const or data declaration. The name is renamed with __ntd$ and
// bound, so a later statement of the same expansion can call it.
fn produce_declaration(env:Env,ctx:Ctx,id:u64,inv:u64,depth:u64)->u64 {
    let n:u64=id;
    if nkind(env,n)==k_fn() { return produce_function(env,ctx,id,inv,depth); }
    if naux(env,n)==1 { return fail(env,377,2,nline(env,n),ncolumn(env,n)); }
    let name_id:u64=kid(env,id);
    let type_id:u64=nth(env,id,1);
    let init_id:u64=nth(env,id,2);
    if live(env,name_id)==false || live(env,type_id)==false ||
       live(env,init_id)==false { return fail(env,800,1,nline(env,n),ncolumn(env,n)); }
    let name:u64=name_id;
    if binding_exists(env,name_id)==true {
        return fail(env,376,1,nline(env,name),ncolumn(env,name));
    }
    if caller_declares(env,nstart(env,name),nend(env,name)) {
        return fail(env,381,1,nline(env,name),ncolumn(env,name));
    }
    if reserved_word(env,name_id) { return fail(env,376,3,nline(env,name),ncolumn(env,name)); }
    let start:u64=mark_decl(env,ctx.ordinal,ctx.serial,ctx.repeat,ctx.item,
        ctx.listed,name,inv);
    if start==0 && env.code!=0 { return 0; }
    if charge_fuel(env,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
    let out:u64=emit(env,nkind(env,n),0,n,inv,nstart(env,inv),nend(env,inv));
    if out==0 { return 0; }
    let length:u64=env.name_bytes-start;
    if push_binding(env,bind_decl(),false,0,start,length,name_id,0)!=0 {
        return 0;
    }
    let fresh:u64=emit_name(env,name,inv,start,length);
    if fresh==0 { return 0; }
    prod_attach(env,out,fresh);
    let type_copy:u64=clone_type(env,ctx,type_id,inv,depth+1);
    if type_copy==0 { return 0; }
    prod_attach(env,out,type_copy);
    let init_copy:u64=clone(env,ctx,init_id,pos_bind(),inv,depth+1);
    if init_copy==0 { return 0; }
    prod_attach(env,out,init_copy);
    return out;
}

// A meta return produces the macro result.
fn run_return(env:Env,ctx:Ctx,id:u64,inv:u64,depth:u64)->u64 {
    let n:u64=id;
    let value_id:u64=kid(env,id);
    if live(env,value_id)==false { return fail(env,800,1,nline(env,n),ncolumn(env,n)); }
    let value:u64=value_id;
    if nkind(env,value)!=k_quote() {
        if ctx.result!=result_expr() { return fail(env,377,3,nline(env,n),ncolumn(env,n)); }
        return clone(env,ctx,value_id,pos_bind(),inv,depth+1);
    }
    if ctx.result==result_decl() { return fail(env,377,3,nline(env,n),ncolumn(env,n)); }
    let kind_id:u64=kid(env,value_id);
    let payload:u64=nth(env,value_id,1);
    if live(env,payload)==false { return fail(env,800,1,nline(env,value),ncolumn(env,value)); }
    if ctx.result==result_expr() && naux(env,value)==1 { return clone(env,ctx,payload,pos_bind(),inv,depth+1); }
    if ctx.result==result_type() && naux(env,value)==4 { return clone_type(env,ctx,payload,inv,depth+1); }
    if ctx.result==result_stmt() && naux(env,value)==2 {
        if live(env,kind_id)==false { return fail(env,800,1,nline(env,value),ncolumn(env,value)); }
        if word_id(env,kind_id,9)==false { return fail(env,372,1,nline(env,value),ncolumn(env,value)); }
        return clone(env,ctx,payload,pos_bind(),inv,depth+1);
    }
    if ctx.result==result_block() && naux(env,value)==5 {
        if word_id(env,kind_id,11)==false { return fail(env,372,1,nline(env,value),ncolumn(env,value)); }
        // A quote(block) holds the statements directly, so the block node is
        // produced here and the statements become its children.
        if charge_fuel(env,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
        let out:u64=emit(env,k_block(),0,value,inv,nstart(env,inv),nend(env,inv));
        if out==0 { return 0; }
        let save:u64=env.bind_top;
        // The statements of a quoted block are the reference's template items
        // one for one, so this is where the reference's 512 applies, and the
        // count is refused rather than shortened.
        let count:u64=template_items(env,value_id,1);
        if env.code!=0 { env.bind_top=save;return 0; }
        let mut index:u64=1;
        while index<count {
            let statement:u64=nth(env,value_id,index);
            let piece:u64=clone(env,ctx,statement,pos_bind(),inv,depth+1);
            if piece==0 { env.bind_top=save;return 0; }
            prod_attach(env,out,piece);
            index=index+1;
        }
        env.bind_top=save;
        return out;
    }
    return fail(env,372,1,nline(env,value),ncolumn(env,value));
}

// A meta emit produces a declaration.
fn run_emit(env:Env,ctx:Ctx,id:u64,inv:u64,depth:u64)->u64 {
    let n:u64=id;
    if ctx.result!=result_decl() { return fail(env,377,4,nline(env,n),ncolumn(env,n)); }
    let value_id:u64=kid(env,id);
    if live(env,value_id)==false { return fail(env,800,1,nline(env,n),ncolumn(env,n)); }
    let value:u64=value_id;
    if nkind(env,value)!=k_quote() || naux(env,value)!=3 {
        return fail(env,372,1,nline(env,value),ncolumn(env,value));
    }
    let kind_id:u64=kid(env,value_id);
    if word_id(env,kind_id,10)==false {
        return fail(env,372,1,nline(env,value),ncolumn(env,value));
    }
    let payload:u64=nth(env,value_id,1);
    if live(env,payload)==false { return fail(env,800,1,nline(env,value),ncolumn(env,value)); }
    let pn:u64=payload;
    if nkind(env,pn)!=k_const() && nkind(env,pn)!=k_data() && nkind(env,pn)!=k_fn() {
        return fail(env,375,1,nline(env,pn),ncolumn(env,pn));
    }
    return produce_declaration(env,ctx,payload,inv,depth+1);
}

// A meta let binds a comptime value, evaluated in order with the arguments
// already spliced, so the value it holds is the folded constant itself.
fn run_meta_let(env:Env,ctx:Ctx,id:u64,inv:u64,depth:u64)->u64 {
    let n:u64=id;
    let name_id:u64=kid(env,id);
    let value_id:u64=nth(env,id,1);
    if live(env,name_id)==false || live(env,value_id)==false {
        return fail(env,800,1,nline(env,n),ncolumn(env,n));
    }
    if binding_exists(env,name_id)==true {
        return fail(env,376,1,nline(env,name_id),ncolumn(env,name_id));
    }
    if parameter_index(env,ctx,name_id)<macro_parameters(env,ctx.macro) {
        return fail(env,376,1,nline(env,name_id),ncolumn(env,name_id));
    }
    if reserved_word(env,name_id) {
        return fail(env,376,3,nline(env,name_id),ncolumn(env,name_id));
    }
    scratch_begin(env);
    let outer_folding:bool=env.folding;
    env.folding=true;
    let copy:u64=clone(env,ctx,value_id,pos_bind(),inv,depth+1);
    env.folding=outer_folding;
    if copy==0 { scratch_end(env);return 0; }
    let value:constant_eval.Value=fold(env,copy);
    scratch_end(env);
    if value.status!=0 {
        if value.status==800 { return fail(env,800,1,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv)); }
        let channel:u64=fold_channel(env,ctx,value_id,0);
        if channel!=0 { return fail(env,379,channel,value.line,value.column); }
        return fail(env,379,value.status,value.line,value.column);
    }
    // value_id is the folded expression in the CALLER tree, and it is the
    // range a materialised $k takes its token from.
    if push_binding(env,bind_value(),value.negative,value.magnitude,0,0,
        name_id,value_id)!=0 { return 0; }
    return 1;
}

// A meta if is decided by the folded condition, except for the two boolean
// literals, which are words and not expressions. The parser does not hand a
// bare word over as a name: primary wraps a NAME token in a kind 35 node of
// aux 0 around a qualified node around the name, so a test on kind 90 never
// matches and the word can only reach the evaluator, which refuses it. This
// unwraps exactly that shape and nothing else: a call, a parenthesised
// expression, a splice and a qualified multi segment name all keep folding.
// Nothing can bind true or false, because reserved_word refuses the name at
// every introducer, so the word always means the word and cannot be made to
// lie.
fn boolean_word(env:Env,id:u64)->u64 {
    if live(env,id)==false { return 0; }
    let n:u64=id;
    if nkind(env,n)!=k_expr() || naux(env,n)!=0 { return 0; }
    if child_count(env,id)!=1 { return 0; }
    let qualified:u64=nth(env,id,0);
    if nkind(env,qualified)!=k_qualified() { return 0; }
    if child_count(env,qualified)!=1 { return 0; }
    let inner:u64=nth(env,qualified,0);
    if word_id(env,inner,21) { return 1; }
    if word_id(env,inner,22) { return 2; }
    return 0;
}

// A meta if is decided by the folded condition.
fn run_meta_if(env:Env,ctx:Ctx,id:u64,inv:u64,depth:u64)->u64 {
    let n:u64=id;
    let cond_id:u64=kid(env,id);
    let then_id:u64=nth(env,id,1);
    let else_id:u64=nth(env,id,2);
    if live(env,cond_id)==false || live(env,then_id)==false {
        return fail(env,800,1,nline(env,n),ncolumn(env,n));
    }
    let cond:u64=cond_id;
    let mut taken:bool=false;
    let word:u64=boolean_word(env,cond_id);
    if word==1 { taken=true; }
    else if word==2 { taken=false; }
    else {
        scratch_begin(env);
        let outer_folding:bool=env.folding;
        env.folding=true;
        let copy:u64=clone(env,ctx,cond_id,pos_bind(),inv,depth+1);
        env.folding=outer_folding;
        if copy==0 { scratch_end(env);return 0; }
        let value:constant_eval.Value=fold(env,copy);
        scratch_end(env);
        if value.status!=0 {
            if value.status==800 { return fail(env,800,1,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv)); }
            let channel:u64=fold_channel(env,ctx,cond_id,0);
            if channel!=0 { return fail(env,379,channel,value.line,value.column); }
            return fail(env,379,value.status,value.line,value.column);
        }
        if value.negative { taken=false; } else { taken=value.magnitude!=0; }
    }
    if taken { return run_block(env,ctx,then_id,inv,depth+1); }
    if live(env,else_id)==true { return run_block(env,ctx,else_id,inv,depth+1); }
    return 0;
}

// A meta for repeats the body. The count is folded, must not be negative and
// is capped, and each repeat binds the loop name to its own index.
fn run_meta_for(env:Env,ctx:Ctx,id:u64,inv:u64,depth:u64)->u64 {
    let n:u64=id;
    let loop_id:u64=kid(env,id);
    let count_id:u64=nth(env,id,1);
    let body_id:u64=nth(env,id,2);
    if live(env,loop_id)==false || live(env,count_id)==false ||
       live(env,body_id)==false { return fail(env,800,1,nline(env,n),ncolumn(env,n)); }
    if binding_exists(env,loop_id)==true {
        return fail(env,376,1,nline(env,loop_id),ncolumn(env,loop_id));
    }
    if reserved_word(env,loop_id) {
        return fail(env,376,3,nline(env,loop_id),ncolumn(env,loop_id));
    }
    scratch_begin(env);
    let outer_folding:bool=env.folding;
    env.folding=true;
    let copy:u64=clone(env,ctx,count_id,pos_bind(),inv,depth+1);
    env.folding=outer_folding;
    if copy==0 { scratch_end(env);return 0; }
    let value:constant_eval.Value=fold(env,copy);
    scratch_end(env);
    if value.status!=0 {
        if value.status==800 { return fail(env,800,1,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv)); }
        let channel:u64=fold_channel(env,ctx,count_id,0);
        if channel!=0 { return fail(env,379,channel,value.line,value.column); }
        return fail(env,379,value.status,value.line,value.column);
    }
    if value.negative { return fail(env,380,1,nline(env,n),ncolumn(env,n)); }
    if value.magnitude>loop_cap() { return refuse_bound(env,380,2,nline(env,n),ncolumn(env,n),value.magnitude,loop_cap()); }
    if charge_fuel(env,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv))!=0 { return 0; }
    let out:u64=emit(env,k_block(),0,n,inv,nstart(env,inv),nend(env,inv));
    if out==0 { return 0; }
    let mut repeat:u64=0;
    while repeat<value.magnitude {
        // The loop index has no token of its own -- it is 0, 1, 2 -- so the
        // count the loop runs to is the only integer the caller wrote for it.
        if push_binding(env,bind_value(),false,repeat,0,0,loop_id,count_id)!=0 {
            return 0;
        }
        let inner:Ctx=ctx;
        inner.repeat=repeat;inner.listed=true;
        let piece:u64=run_block(env,inner,body_id,inv,depth+1);
        if piece==0 { return 0; }
        prod_attach(env,out,piece);
        repeat=repeat+1;
    }
    return out;
}

// One meta block. Exactly one item may produce the result; a second one is a
// macro form outside this profile rather than a silent choice.
fn run_block(env:Env,ctx:Ctx,block:u64,inv:u64,depth:u64)->u64 {
    if depth>walk_depth_cap() { return refuse_bound(env,375,9,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv),depth,walk_depth_cap()); }
    if live(env,block)==false { return fail(env,800,1,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv)); }
    if nkind(env,block)!=k_meta_block() { return fail(env,375,1,nline(env,inv),ncolumn(env,inv)); }
    let save:u64=env.bind_top;
    let mut result:u64=0;
    let mut at_id:u64=kid(env,block);
    while at_id!=0 {
        if env.code!=0 { env.bind_top=save;return 0; }
        let n:u64=at_id;
        let mut piece:u64=0;
        if nkind(env,n)==k_meta_let() { piece=run_meta_let(env,ctx,at_id,inv,depth+1); }
        else if nkind(env,n)==k_meta_if() { piece=run_meta_if(env,ctx,at_id,inv,depth+1); }
        else if nkind(env,n)==k_meta_for() { piece=run_meta_for(env,ctx,at_id,inv,depth+1); }
        else if nkind(env,n)==k_meta_return() { piece=run_return(env,ctx,at_id,inv,depth+1); }
        else if nkind(env,n)==k_meta_emit() { piece=run_emit(env,ctx,at_id,inv,depth+1); }
        else { env.bind_top=save;return fail(env,375,1,nline(env,n),ncolumn(env,n)); }
        if env.code!=0 { env.bind_top=save;return 0; }
        if piece>1 {
            if result!=0 { env.bind_top=save;return fail(env,377,5,nline(env,n),ncolumn(env,n)); }
            result=piece;
        }
        at_id=sib(env,at_id);
    }
    env.bind_top=save;
    return result;
}

// Expand one expand invocation into a fresh region. The wrapper is the first
// produced node of the expansion, so the produced ids stay in lexical order.
fn expand_node(env:Env,parent:u64,inv_id:u64,depth:u64)->u64 {
    if live(env,inv_id)==false { return 0; }
    let inv_node:u64=inv_id;
    if nkind(env,inv_node)!=k_expand() { return fail(env,375,1,nline(env,inv_node),ncolumn(env,inv_node)); }
    if env.depth>=env.depth_limit {
        return refuse_bound(env,201,budget_depth(),nline(env,inv_node),ncolumn(env,inv_node),
                            env.depth+1,env.depth_limit);
    }
    if env.call_depth>=env.call_limit {
        return refuse_bound(env,201,budget_call(),nline(env,inv_node),ncolumn(env,inv_node),
                            env.call_depth+1,env.call_limit);
    }
    if charge_fuel(env,nline(env,inv_node),ncolumn(env,inv_node))!=0 { return 0; }
    env.depth=env.depth+1;env.call_depth=env.call_depth+1;
    if env.depth>env.depth_peak { env.depth_peak=env.depth; }
    if env.call_depth>env.call_peak { env.call_peak=env.call_depth; }
    env.expansions=env.expansions+1;
    // A nested expansion reached from a folded condition publishes its own
    // wrapper, so the temporary region is closed around it.
    let outer_scratch:bool=env.scratch;
    env.scratch=false;env.scratch_used=0;
    let wrap_id:u64=emit(env,k_expand(),0,inv_node,inv_node,nstart(env,inv_node),nend(env,inv_node));
    if wrap_id==0 { return 0; }
    let name_id:u64=kid(env,inv_id);
    if live(env,name_id)==false { return fail(env,800,1,nline(env,inv_node),ncolumn(env,inv_node)); }
    let macro_id:u64=find_macro(env,name_id);
    if macro_id==0 {
        return fail(env,370,1,nline(env,name_id),ncolumn(env,name_id));
    }
    let ordinal:u64=macro_ordinal(env,macro_id);
    if ordinal==0 { return fail(env,370,2,nline(env,inv_node),ncolumn(env,inv_node)); }
    // The wrapper names the macro it came from, so a consumer can find the
    // roots of the produced region without a side table.
    env.node_words[(wrap_id-1)*12+11]=ordinal;
    let result_kind:u64=macro_result(env,macro_id);
    if result_kind==0 {
        return fail(env,377,1,nline(env,macro_id),ncolumn(env,macro_id));
    }
    let count:u64=macro_parameters(env,macro_id);
    if count>parameter_cap() {
        return refuse_bound(env,372,2,nline(env,macro_id),ncolumn(env,macro_id),
                            count,parameter_cap());
    }
    let given:u64=child_count(env,inv_id)-1;
    if given!=count {
        return fail(env,371,1,nline(env,inv_node),ncolumn(env,inv_node));
    }
    let mut ctx:Ctx=empty_ctx();
    ctx.macro=macro_id;ctx.ordinal=ordinal;ctx.result=result_kind;
    ctx.serial=env.serial+1;
    env.serial=ctx.serial;
    let arg_base:u64=env.arg_top;
    ctx.arg_base=arg_base;ctx.frame=env.depth;
    if ensure_frames(env)!=0 { return 0; }
    let frames:buf<u64>=env.frames;
    if parent!=0 {
        if parent>=env.depth { return fail(env,800,1,nline(env,inv_node),ncolumn(env,inv_node)); }
        frames[(parent-1)*4+3]=env.bind_top;
    }
    let frame_at:u64=(env.depth-1)*4;
    frames[frame_at]=ctx.macro;frames[frame_at+1]=arg_base;
    frames[frame_at+2]=env.bind_top;frames[frame_at+3]=env.bind_top;
    let mut index:u64=0;
    while index<count {
        let param:u64=macro_parameter_at(env,macro_id,index);
        let kind:u64=parameter_kind(env,param);
        if kind==0 {
            return fail(env,372,3,nline(env,param),ncolumn(env,param));
        }
        let argument:u64=nth(env,inv_id,index+1);
        if live(env,argument)==false {
            return fail(env,800,1,nline(env,inv_node),ncolumn(env,inv_node));
        }
        let an:u64=argument;
        if kind==1 || kind==3 {
            if nkind(env,an)==k_quote() {
                return fail(env,372,4,nline(env,an),ncolumn(env,an));
            }
            if kind==3 {
                let mut negative:bool=false;
                let mut magnitude:u64=0;
                if nkind(env,an)==k_literal() { magnitude=naux(env,an); }
                else if nkind(env,an)==k_expr() && naux(env,an)==45 {
                    // A comptime value parameter also accepts the unary minus
                    // of a plain literal, so a negative count or offset is
                    // expressible without a second evaluator.
                    let inner:u64=kid(env,argument);
                    if live(env,inner)==false || nkind(env,inner)!=k_literal() {
                        return fail(env,372,5,nline(env,an),ncolumn(env,an));
                    }
                    if nth(env,argument,1)!=0 {
                        return fail(env,372,5,nline(env,an),ncolumn(env,an));
                    }
                    negative=true;magnitude=naux(env,inner);
                } else if nkind(env,an)==k_name() {
                    if word_id(env,argument,21) { magnitude=1; }
                    else if word_id(env,argument,22) { magnitude=0; }
                    else { return fail(env,372,5,nline(env,an),ncolumn(env,an)); }
                } else { return fail(env,372,5,nline(env,an),ncolumn(env,an)); }
                if value_fits(env,parameter_type_node(env,param),negative,
                    magnitude)==false {
                    return fail(env,372,6,nline(env,an),ncolumn(env,an));
                }
                if push_value_arg(env,negative,magnitude,argument)!=0 { return 0; }
            } else if parent!=0 {
                if push_arg(env,3,argument,parent,argument)!=0 { return 0; }
            } else if argument_name(env,an)!=0 &&
                      binding_exists(env,argument_name(env,an))==true {
                // A NAME THE ENCLOSING TEMPLATE BOUND is not a caller name,
                // and this is the one place that can still tell the two apart.
                // The binding stack is at its fullest here: the outer template
                // has pushed the binding this name resolves to, and
                // expand_node has not yet raised scope_base above it. One
                // line later the nested expansion runs with scope_base ABOVE
                // these entries, so by the time the splice asks the question
                // the answer is gone.
                //
                // WHAT WENT WRONG WITHOUT THIS. The argument was pushed as a
                // plain node, the splice copied its SPELLING verbatim, and the
                // inner expansion published a bare `t` while the outer
                // published `__ntm$2$e1$t`. In a module whose caller also
                // binds `t` at function scope -- the measured edge of 381,
                // which declares_below does not read because k_let is not
                // among its four kinds -- that bare `t` binds to the CALLER's
                // declaration. spec/ntasm/ntasm.md 13 forbids the capture by
                // default, so the correct answer is the mark.
                let aname:u64=argument_name(env,an);
                let slot2:u64=bind_offset(env,aname,true,true,true);
                if slot2==0 { return fail(env,373,1,nline(env,aname),ncolumn(env,aname)); }
                let value2:u64=slot2-1;
                if push_mark_arg(env,bind_start(env,value2),
                                 bind_length(env,value2),argument)!=0 { return 0; }
            } else if push_node_arg(env,argument)!=0 { return 0; }
        } else {
            if nkind(env,an)!=k_quote() || naux(env,an)!=4 {
                return fail(env,372,4,nline(env,an),ncolumn(env,an));
            }
            let inner:u64=nth(env,argument,1);
            if live(env,inner)==false { return fail(env,800,1,nline(env,an),ncolumn(env,an)); }
            if nkind(env,inner)!=k_type() {
                return fail(env,372,4,nline(env,an),ncolumn(env,an));
            }
            if parent!=0 { if push_arg(env,3,inner,parent,inner)!=0 { return 0; } }
            else if push_node_arg(env,inner)!=0 { return 0; }
        }
        index=index+1;
    }
    let body:u64=macro_body(env,macro_id);
    if body==0 { return fail(env,377,6,nline(env,macro_id),ncolumn(env,macro_id)); }
    // A nested expansion is a fresh template scope. Its bindings go above the
    // ones the opening expansion already made, so a push never overwrites
    // them, and the lookups start above them, so a name written in the nested
    // template resolves against the nested template alone. run_block pops
    // what the nested body binds, and the scope base is put back before the
    // result is attached, so a statement after the nested expansion still
    // reads the outer bindings. The two marks differ by serial anyway, so
    // nothing here weakens the hygiene: it is what makes a nested expansion
    // able to use a name the opening expansion already used.
    let outer_scope:u64=env.scope_base;
    let outer_bind:u64=env.bind_top;
    env.scope_base=outer_bind;
    let made:u64=run_block(env,ctx,body,inv_node,0);
    env.bind_top=outer_bind;
    env.scope_base=outer_scope;
    // Every supported macro form produces exactly one result, so an empty
    // expansion is a form outside this profile rather than an empty AST.
    if made==0 {
        if env.code==0 { return fail(env,377,7,nline(env,inv_node),ncolumn(env,inv_node)); }
        return 0;
    }
    prod_attach(env,wrap_id,made);
    env.arg_top=arg_base;
    env.depth=env.depth-1;env.call_depth=env.call_depth-1;
    env.scratch=outer_scratch;
    if outer_scratch==true { env.scratch_used=0; }
    return wrap_id;
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------ caller walk
// Lexical and stable: the module tree is visited in node order, a comptime or
// macro declaration is not descended into, and an expand is expanded where it
// stands.
fn walk(env:Env,id:u64,depth:u64)->u64 {
    if depth>walk_depth_cap() { return refuse_bound(env,375,9,0,0,depth,walk_depth_cap()); }
    if live(env,id)==false { return 0; }
    if env.code!=0 { return 0; }
    // NO FUEL IS CHARGED HERE, and that is the alignment, not an omission.
    //
    // This walk used to charge one unit for every node of the CALLER's module.
    // The reference charges nothing for the caller's module at all: its
    // consume_fuel is called from clone_macro_expression, expand_macro_statement,
    // expand_macro_value and the declaration-expansion path, and from nowhere in
    // the parser's own descent. frontend.c has no counterpart to this line.
    //
    // Measured on the same text through both, in a directory named for it:
    // a module with a section, a function, a body and a macro declaration and
    // NO expansion costs the reference 1 unit, all of it the lump its own
    // comptime body is charged, and costs this expander 23. On the corpus
    // member it is 5 against 30, and the gate's own SHADOW ladder prints the
    // constant this line was: 27 units at zero data items.
    //
    // So the two schemes were never comparable as totals, and the ratio is not
    // even constant -- 23:1 with no expansion, 30:5 with one, 2565:1 at 508
    // data items on a differently shaped ladder. No single rate maps one onto
    // the other, which is why this is a removal of a charge the oracle does not
    // make and not a change of rate.
    //
    // WHAT THIS DOES NOT SETTLE, and the next thing to measure: with the walk
    // silent the corpus member costs 8 against the reference's 5. The residual
    // is two divergences, not one, and they are additive rather than
    // overlapping. This expansion charges 8 where the reference charges 4, and
    // it charges NOTHING for the comptime block's own body where the reference
    // charges the expression count it parsed there and can refuse with
    // "comptime evaluation exceeds fuel" -- a refusal this expander cannot
    // produce at all. Neither is settled by this line.
    let n:u64=id;
    if nkind(env,n)==k_comptime() || nkind(env,n)==k_macro() { return 0; }
    if nkind(env,n)==k_type() && naux(env,n)==3 { return fail(env,386,1,nline(env,n),ncolumn(env,n)); }
    if nkind(env,n)==k_expand() { return expand_node(env,0,id,depth); }
    let mut at_id:u64=kid(env,id);
    while at_id!=0 {
        if env.code!=0 { return 0; }
        walk(env,at_id,depth+1);
        at_id=sib(env,at_id);
    }
    return 0;
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------- core
// The budget block is applied before anything is expanded, so the limits in
// force are the ones the module declares. A module with no comptime block runs
// on the frozen maxima.
fn run(source:buf<u8>,tree:ntasm_ast.Tree,report:bool,out:buf<u64>)->u64 {
    let env:Env=fresh_env(source,tree);
    if tree.ok==false || tree.node_count==0 {
        release_env(env);return 800;
    }
    let mut code:u64=0;
    if report==true {
        env.fuel_limit=max_fuel();env.call_limit=max_call_depth();
        env.arena_limit=max_arena();env.node_limit=max_ast_nodes();
        env.depth_limit=max_expansion_depth();env.constant_limit=max_constant_bytes();
    }
    if len(out)<4 { release_env(env);return 800; }
    let mut at_id:u64=kid(env,1);
    while at_id!=0 && code==0 {
        // A measure run ignores the declared block on purpose: it reports the
        // cost of the expansion under the frozen maxima, so a caller can then
        // pin a declared budget at that exact cost.
        if nkind(env,at_id)==k_comptime() && report==false {
            code=apply_comptime(env,at_id);
        }
        at_id=sib(env,at_id);
    }
    if code==0 { env.macros=count_macros(env); }
    if code==0 { walk(env,1,0); }
    if env.code!=0 { code=env.code; }
    if code==0 && report==true {
        if len(out)<10 { release_env(env);return 800; }
        out[0]=0;out[1]=env.fuel;out[2]=env.call_peak;out[3]=env.arena;
        out[4]=env.node_count;out[5]=env.depth_peak;out[6]=env.constant;
        out[7]=env.name_bytes;out[8]=env.expansions;out[9]=env.macros;
        release_env(env);return 0;
    }
    if code==0 {
        // Publish only after the whole expansion succeeded, so a refusal can
        // never leave a partial AST in the caller buffer.
        let name_words:u64=(env.name_bytes+7)/8;
        let need:u64=4+name_words+env.node_count*12;
        if len(out)<need {
            out[0]=203;out[1]=inv_line(env);out[2]=1;out[3]=need;
            release_env(env);return 203;
        }
        out[0]=0;out[1]=env.node_count;out[2]=env.name_bytes;out[3]=0;
        let mut i:u64=0;
        while i<name_words { out[4+i]=env.name_words[i];i=i+1; }
        let mut id:u64=1;
        while id<=env.node_count {
            let s:u64=(id-1)*12;
            let d:u64=4+name_words+s;
            let mut k:u64=0;
            while k<12 { out[d+k]=env.node_words[s+k];k=k+1; }
            id=id+1;
        }
        release_env(env);return 0;
    }
    if code==203 { release_env(env);return 203; }
    out[0]=code;
    if env.line==0 { out[1]=1; } else { out[1]=env.line; }
    out[2]=env.column;out[3]=env.reason;
    // out[10] and out[11], and ONLY when the caller left room for twelve
    // words. A four-word caller gets byte-identical behaviour to the ABI it
    // was written against: the two words are the whole of the addition and
    // the guard is what makes that true. See the export's own comment for
    // what they name and why they are HERE and not at out[4] and out[5].
    if len(out)>=12 { out[10]=env.offending;out[11]=env.limit; }
    release_env(env);return code;
}

// The invocation line used for a capacity refusal, which is not a budget.
fn inv_line(env:Env)->u64 {
    let mut scan:u64=1;
    while scan<=env.tree.node_count {
        if nkind(env,scan)==k_expand() { return nline(env,scan); }
        scan=scan+1;
    }
    return 1;
}

// Expand every macro invocation of a parsed module. On success out[0] is zero,
// out[1] is the produced node count, out[2] is the name table byte length and
// the produced region follows at out[4]: the name table first, then twelve
// words per produced node with one based ids. On a refusal out[0] is the
// diagnostic code, out[1] the line, out[2] the column, out[3] the reason, and
// no produced region is written. A refusal therefore never publishes a partial
// AST, as spec/ntasm/ntasm.md 13 requires.
//
// ON A REFUSAL, AND ONLY ON A REFUSAL, out[10] and out[11] mean:
//
//   out[10]  offending  the QUANTITY that crossed the cap, in the cap's OWN
//                        unit -- not in any unit the body happens to be
//                        written in. A macro body of 31 nested levels that
//                        walks past walk_depth_cap publishes the walk depth
//                        that crossed it, which is not 31 and is not
//                        env.depth_peak (that is EXPANSION nesting, a
//                        different recursion raised on a different path).
//   out[11]  limit      the declared cap it crossed, in the same unit.
//
// So a bound refusal reads out[11] > 0 with out[10] > out[11] -- 9 against 8,
// 65 against 64 -- and a refusal that is NOT a bound (a dead id, a wrong node
// kind, a literal out of range) publishes 0 and 0, which is how the two are
// told apart.
//
// WHY TEN AND ELEVEN AND NOT FOUR AND FIVE.  This is the second attempt at
// this position and the first one was wrong in a way worth recording.
// out[4] and out[5] are already spoken for: on a successful MEASURE they are
// env.node_count and env.depth_peak.  Publishing offending/limit there would
// have made one position carry two meanings, disambiguated only by whether
// out[0] is zero -- a caller would have to hold the code to read the data,
// and a channel that can only be decoded by something the reader already
// holds is not a channel.  Nothing else writes words 10 and 11 on any path:
// a success measure writes out[0..9], and the produced region begins at
// out[4] only when out[0] is zero.  So ten and eleven mean offending/limit
// and nothing else, and POSITION ALONE decodes them.  Both are written only
// under `len(out)>=12`.
//
// Two refusals never reach the twelve words at all, and both say why.  800 is
// returned when the caller's out holds fewer than four words, and it writes
// NOTHING into out -- there is no room to write a code into.  203 writes
// out[0..3] and then returns, before the quantity words, because it is the
// caller's buffer being too small for a produced region and not a bound
// refusing on anything.  Neither is a bound refusal, so neither invents a
// quantity to look like one.
//
// There is NO text, and there is still none after this change.  out is
// buf<u64> and every word of it is a number, so a "message" here is a number
// a formatter would render on the caller's side.  Nothing in this module
// formats, and a scan of the whole out region of a refusal for printable byte
// runs of four or more finds none.  That is a property of the ABI, not a
// missing formatter: what this adds is two more NUMBERS, which is what a
// numeric channel can carry.
export fn expand(source:buf<u8>,tree:ntasm_ast.Tree,out:buf<u64>)->u64 {
    return run(source,tree,false,out);
}

// The same expansion under the frozen maxima, reporting the six consumptions
// so a caller can pin a budget at its exact cost and at one less.
export fn measure(source:buf<u8>,tree:ntasm_ast.Tree,out:buf<u64>)->u64 {
    return run(source,tree,true,out);
}
